const HELP_DOCTOR = `colab doctor [--prune] [--ttl <hours>] [--json] [--sync]

Health-check the machine-local state and heal drift. This is the RECORD-points-nowhere
direction; the mirror direction — a directory that exists with NO record — is reported by
\`colab worktrees\` itself (its "unrecorded" section, #67), not here: doctor's fixes read and
write colab's OWN state, and a directory with no record has no state entry to prune ports or
claims from.
  - A worktree whose directory no longer exists on disk is DEAD: its claims are pruned
    and its ports freed (--prune actually removes; default only reports).
  - Claims referencing a worktree that isn't in state are orphans (same treatment).
  - Worktree-less claims older than TTL (default 24h, --ttl to change) are REPORTED, not
    auto-removed (they may be deliberate trunk claims). --prune removes them too and frees
    any ports they own.
  - Ports owned by a vanished worktree/claim are freed on --prune.
  - A record nothing can ACT on is drift too: a branch that is a role word (\`trunk\`) or resolves
    to no ref, or a worktree naming no directory. Not the same as DEAD — dead means the directory
    is gone, these name none — which is why it went unreported while \`landed\` answered unknown and
    \`ship\` merged with no \`Closes #N\`. --prune repairs only what a directory on disk proves
    (adopting its path and its checked-out branch); a branch nothing can see is never guessed.
  - A \`running\` worktree whose branch has landed on trunk (tree-contained, incl. squash merges) AND
    holds NO live claims is flipped to \`merged\` (recorded on --prune). ANY live claim keeps it
    running (an unfinished group sibling). \`merged\` worktrees still on disk are sweep candidates;
    --prune tears them down fully (pre-remove hook, git worktree remove, free ports) — but refuses
    ones with uncommitted TRACKED changes, reporting them instead. \`running\` worktrees are NEVER
    swept.
  - Branches whose content is already in trunk are listed as ROUTINE MAINTENANCE, after the health
    verdict and never counted as drift: \`ship\` keeps the branch by default, so one accumulates per
    shipped session by design. doctor never deletes a ref — not even with --prune — it prints the
    commands. \`git branch --merged\` cannot find these (a squash leaves no ancestry), so nothing
    else will. Branches checked out in a worktree are excluded (a live session's branch is not
    spent), and so are branches that CONFLICT against trunk rather than matching it — the list is
    honest rather than exhaustive. Also excluded: trunk, \`main\`, any declared \`integration:\` line,
    and a declared \`releaseBranch:\` (project.schema.md) — an external-deploy branch that sits
    "contained in trunk" between releases BY CONSTRUCTION, which this heuristic would otherwise
    misread as spent and advise deleting, silently breaking GitOps deploys (#63).

This exists because claims made without a worktree were never auto-healed before, and a
real repo accumulated 17 stale same-day claims. Run \`colab doctor\` regularly; \`--prune\`
in a cleanup cron.

  --prune   apply the fixes (default: report only)
  --ttl N   hours before a worktree-less claim is flagged stale (default 24)
  --json    machine-readable report
  --sync    ALSO check each repo's tracker for spent \`group:<key>\` labels (#85). Off by
            default because it is the only network work doctor does — one \`gh label list\`
            per repo, plus one membership call per group label — and doctor is otherwise
            fleet-wide and instant. Same opt-in shape as \`colab claims --sync\`.
            With --prune, deletes the labels found SPENT (every member closed) — the
            back-fill for groups \`ship\`'s B4 teardown can never reach: ones spent before
            that feature existed, or closed by any route other than \`ship\`. A group with
            an open member is mid-flight and is never reported; a group with NO members is
            reported but never deleted (a label a triage session just created and has not
            applied yet is indistinguishable from one whose members were all transferred
            away — the labels API exposes no creation time, so a human decides).
`;

const EMPTY_SET = new Set();

/**
 * Local branches whose content is already in trunk — the routine cleanup list.
 *
 * This is the PRIMARY mechanism, not a backstop. `ship` keeps the branch by default (deleting refs
 * from a shared remote is not an agent's call), so a shipped branch accumulates per session by
 * design and this list is expected to be non-empty. `git branch --merged <trunk>` cannot find any
 * of them: sessions squash-merge, which leaves the branch tip outside trunk's ancestry, so the
 * standard cleanup check reports clean forever. We reuse branchContainedInTrunk (a merge-tree
 * content test) which is correct across squashes, and skip anything checked out in a worktree — a
 * live session's branch is not spent, and an empty branch freshly cut from trunk is "contained" by
 * construction.
 *
 * KNOWN HOLE, stated because a cleanup list that quietly under-reports is worse than none: a branch
 * whose work trunk has since REWRITTEN conflicts against trunk rather than matching it, so
 * branchContainedInTrunk answers "not contained" and it never appears here. That is the safe
 * direction to be wrong in — the alternative is telling someone unmerged work is spent — but it
 * means this list is honest, not exhaustive, and a periodic human sweep is still warranted. We hit
 * exactly this case cleaning up after issue #17.
 *
 * REPORT ONLY, even under --prune. doctor's other prunes touch only colab's own state file;
 * deleting refs across every repo a shared machine happens to know about is categorically different.
 */
function shippedBranches(repos, st) {
  const out = [];
  // repo -> its live session branches. A NESTED map, not a composite string key: a flat key needs
  // a separator, and the only separator that cannot collide with a path or a ref name is NUL --
  // which, typed raw, silently turned this file into 'binary data' (grep found nothing without
  // -a, while node --check and git diff stayed happy). Nesting needs no separator at all, so the
  // whole class of bug is gone rather than escaped around.
  const liveByRepo = new Map();
  for (const w of Object.values(st.worktrees)) {
    if (!liveByRepo.has(w.repo)) liveByRepo.set(w.repo, new Set());
    liveByRepo.get(w.repo).add(w.branch);
  }
  for (const repo of repos) {
    if (!repo || !fs.existsSync(repo) || !git.repoRoot(repo)) continue;
    const doc = projectDoc(repo);
    const trunk = resolveTrunk(repo, doc);
    if (!trunk) continue;
    // A declared integration line is never "spent": it is an integration point that outlives many
    // sessions, and it is contained in trunk by construction for as long as nothing has been merged
    // into it yet — which is exactly when suggesting its deletion would be most destructive.
    const lines = resolveIntegration(repo, doc, trunk);
    // A declared external-deploy release branch is never "spent" either, for the opposite reason: it
    // is CONSUMED, not accumulated into. Between releases it sits fast-forwarded to trunk's tip as of
    // the last tag, so it reads as fully "contained in trunk" — the exact shape this list hunts for —
    // right up until the moment deleting it would silently stop production deploys (#63).
    const release = resolveReleaseBranch(repo, doc, trunk);
    // Branches checked out anywhere in this repo (main checkout + every worktree) are off limits.
    const checkedOut = new Set(
      git.git(['worktree', 'list', '--porcelain'], repo).stdout.split('\n')
        .filter((l) => l.startsWith('branch '))
        .map((l) => l.slice('branch '.length).replace(/^refs\/heads\//, '')),
    );
    const refs = git.git(['for-each-ref', '--format=%(refname:short)', 'refs/heads/'], repo);
    if (!refs.ok) continue;
    for (const branch of refs.stdout.split('\n').filter(Boolean)) {
      if (branch === trunk || branch === 'main' || branch === release || lines.includes(branch)) continue;
      if (checkedOut.has(branch)) continue;
      if ((liveByRepo.get(repo) || EMPTY_SET).has(branch)) continue;
      if (!branchContainedInTrunk(repo, branch).contained) continue;
      const onRemote = git.git(['rev-parse', '--verify', '--quiet', `refs/remotes/origin/${branch}`], repo).ok;
      out.push({ repo: shortRepo(repo), repoAbs: repo, branch, trunk, remote: onRemote });
    }
  }
  return out;
}

function cmdDoctor(argv) {
  const opts = parseArgs(argv, {
    values: { '--ttl': 'ttl' }, flags: { '--prune': 'prune', '--json': 'json', '--sync': 'sync', '-h': 'help', '--help': 'help' },
  });
  if (opts.help) return print(HELP_DOCTOR);
  const config = state.loadConfig();
  const ttl = opts.ttl ? parseFloat(opts.ttl) : config.claimTTLHours;

  const report = { deadWorktrees: [], orphanClaims: [], staleClaims: [], orphanPorts: [], brokenRecords: [], prunedPorts: [],
    mergedFlips: [], mergedSweep: [], mergedDirty: [], mergedHookFail: [], mergedRemoveFail: [], shippedBranches: [], ghostPorts: [],
    spentGroupLabels: [], groupLabelsChecked: !!opts.sync, pruned: !!opts.prune };

  // One answer per (repo, branch) — pass 5b asks about the same branch from a worktree and from each
  // of its claims, and every miss costs two `git rev-parse` calls.
  // NESTED, not a composite string key — for the reason spelled out above shippedBranches(): the
  // only separator that cannot collide with a path or a ref name is NUL, and a raw NUL turns this
  // file into "binary data". Nesting needs no separator, so the bug class is gone rather than dodged.
  const refCache = new Map();
  const resolves = (repo, branch) => {
    if (!refCache.has(repo)) refCache.set(repo, new Map());
    const byBranch = refCache.get(repo);
    if (!byBranch.has(branch)) byBranch.set(branch, git.branchExists(repo, branch));
    return byBranch.get(branch);
  };
  const repoLive = (r) => !!r && fs.existsSync(r) && !!git.repoRoot(r);

  const apply = (fn) => opts.prune ? state.mutate(fn) : fn(state.loadState());

  apply((st) => {
    // 1. dead worktrees (path gone)
    for (const wt of Object.values(st.worktrees)) {
      const dead = wt.path && !fs.existsSync(wt.path);
      if (!dead) continue;
      const claims = Object.entries(st.claims).filter(([, c]) => c.worktree === wt.name);
      report.deadWorktrees.push({ name: wt.name, repo: shortRepo(wt.repo), path: wt.path, ports: wt.ports, claims: claims.map(([, c]) => c.issue), session: wt.session || '', sessionName: wt.sessionName || '' });
      if (opts.prune) {
        for (const [k] of claims) delete st.claims[k];
        for (const p of wt.ports) { delete st.ports[String(p)]; report.prunedPorts.push(p); }
        delete st.worktrees[wt.name];
      }
    }
    // 2. orphan claims (worktree ref not in state)
    for (const [k, c] of Object.entries(st.claims)) {
      if (c.worktree && !st.worktrees[c.worktree]) {
        report.orphanClaims.push({ issue: c.issue, repo: shortRepo(c.repo), worktree: c.worktree });
        if (opts.prune) delete st.claims[k];
      }
    }
    // 3. stale worktree-less claims (report only; prune if asked)
    for (const [k, c] of Object.entries(st.claims)) {
      if (!c.worktree) {
        const age = hoursSince(c.created);
        if (age > ttl) {
          report.staleClaims.push({ issue: c.issue, repo: shortRepo(c.repo), ageHours: Math.round(age), host: c.host, session: c.session || '', sessionName: c.sessionName || '' });
          if (opts.prune) {
            // free any ports owned by this claim
            for (const p of Object.keys(st.ports)) {
              if (st.ports[p].owner.type === 'claim' && st.ports[p].owner.ref === k) { delete st.ports[p]; report.prunedPorts.push(Number(p)); }
            }
            delete st.claims[k];
          }
        }
      }
    }
    // 4. orphan ports (owner vanished)
    for (const [p, rec] of Object.entries(st.ports)) {
      const o = rec.owner;
      const orphan = (o.type === 'worktree' && !st.worktrees[o.ref]) || (o.type === 'claim' && !st.claims[o.ref]);
      if (orphan) {
        report.orphanPorts.push({ port: Number(p), owner: `${o.type}:${o.ref}` });
        if (opts.prune) { delete st.ports[p]; report.prunedPorts.push(Number(p)); }
      }
    }
    // 5. running worktrees whose branch has landed on trunk AND that hold no live claims → merged.
    //    ANY live claim (an unfinished group sibling) keeps it running, regardless of branch content.
    for (const wt of Object.values(st.worktrees)) {
      if (wtStatus(wt) !== 'running') continue;
      if (!wt.path || !fs.existsSync(wt.path)) continue; // dead handled in pass 1; stubs have no tree
      const liveClaims = Object.values(st.claims).filter((c) => c.worktree === wt.name).length;
      if (liveClaims > 0) continue;
      // Against the base this worktree was CUT FROM. A session on a declared integration line has
      // shipped when its content is on the line; measuring it against trunk would keep it "running"
      // for as long as the line lives.
      const res = branchContainedInTrunk(wt.repo, wt.branch, wt.base);
      if (res.contained) {
        report.mergedFlips.push({ name: wt.name, repo: shortRepo(wt.repo), branch: wt.branch });
        if (opts.prune) wt.status = 'merged';
      }
    }
    // 5b. records nothing can ACT on (#53): a branch that is a role word or resolves to no ref, and
    //     a worktree that names no directory. Neither shape is "dead" — dead means the directory is
    //     GONE, and these records claim none — so pass 1 looked straight past them while `landed`
    //     answered `unknown` and `ship` matched zero claims and merged with no `Closes #N`.
    for (const wt of Object.values(st.worktrees)) {
      const problems = [];
      let repairTo = null;
      const bp = records.branchProblem(wt.branch);
      if (bp) problems.push(bp);
      else if (wt.branch && repoLive(wt.repo) && !resolves(wt.repo, wt.branch)) {
        problems.push(`branch "${wt.branch}" resolves to no ref in ${shortRepo(wt.repo)}`);
      }
      if (!wt.path) {
        const expected = wt.repo ? path.join(wt.repo, worktreeSubdir(config), wt.name) : null;
        if (expected && fs.existsSync(expected)) {
          problems.push(`path is null while ${expected} exists on disk`);
          repairTo = expected;
        } else if (records.statusOf(wt) !== records.PENDING) {
          problems.push(`path is null while status is "${records.statusOf(wt)}" — it names no directory`);
        } else if (hoursSince(wt.created) > ttl) {
          problems.push(`${records.PENDING} claim stub ${Math.round(hoursSince(wt.created))}h old — the worktree was never created`);
        }
      }
      if (!problems.length) continue;
      const entry = { kind: 'worktree', name: wt.name, repo: shortRepo(wt.repo), branch: wt.branch, problems, repaired: null };
      // Repair ONLY what the disk proves: adopt the directory and the branch actually checked out in
      // it. A branch nothing can see is never guessed — that record is reported and left alone.
      if (opts.prune && repairTo) {
        const head = git.git(['rev-parse', '--abbrev-ref', 'HEAD'], repairTo);
        const real = head.ok ? head.stdout : '';
        if (real && real !== 'HEAD' && !records.branchProblem(real)) {
          wt.path = repairTo;
          wt.branch = real;
          if (records.statusOf(wt) === records.PENDING) wt.status = 'running';
          for (const c of Object.values(st.claims)) {
            if (c.worktree === wt.name && (!c.branch || records.branchProblem(c.branch))) c.branch = real;
          }
          entry.repaired = { path: repairTo, branch: real };
        }
      }
      report.brokenRecords.push(entry);
    }
    for (const c of Object.values(st.claims)) {
      const bp = records.branchProblem(c.branch) ||
        (c.branch && repoLive(c.repo) && !resolves(c.repo, c.branch)
          ? `branch "${c.branch}" resolves to no ref in ${shortRepo(c.repo)}` : null);
      if (!bp) continue;
      report.brokenRecords.push({ kind: 'claim', name: c.issue, repo: shortRepo(c.repo), branch: c.branch, problems: [bp], repaired: null });
    }
  });

  // 6. merged worktrees still on disk = sweep candidates. Report always; --prune tears them down
  //    (pre-remove hook → git worktree remove → free ports → drop entry). Done OUTSIDE the pass-1..5
  //    lock to avoid nesting state.mutate; refuse ones holding ANY uncommitted work — tracked
  //    changes or untracked files (#86) — and report them instead. running worktrees are NEVER
  //    touched by this sweep. This is the unattended path, so the untracked half matters more here
  //    than in `worktree rm`: nobody is watching a --prune sweep decide.
  {
    const st2 = state.loadState();
    for (const wt of Object.values(st2.worktrees)) {
      if (wtStatus(wt) !== 'merged') continue;
      if (!wt.path || !fs.existsSync(wt.path)) continue;
      const work = uncommittedWork(wt.path);
      if (work.any) { report.mergedDirty.push({ name: wt.name, repo: shortRepo(wt.repo), what: work.what }); continue; }
      report.mergedSweep.push({ name: wt.name, repo: shortRepo(wt.repo), ports: wt.ports });
      if (!opts.prune) continue;
      const attached = Object.values(st2.claims).filter((c) => c.worktree === wt.name).map((c) => normIssueNum(c.issue));
      const hook = runHook(wt.repo, 'pre-remove', { wtPath: wt.path, name: wt.name, branch: wt.branch, ports: wt.ports, issues: attached });
      if (hook.ran && !hook.ok) { report.mergedHookFail.push(wt.name); continue; } // pre-remove veto → leave it
      // #62: the removal result is checked, same as cmdWorktreeRm — this loop used to call
      // `git worktree remove` and drop the state record regardless of whether it actually succeeded,
      // which is exactly how a `--prune` sweep turned a failed-or-hung removal into a registry entry
      // that quietly vanished while the husk (and, worse, the claim it should have released) stayed
      // behind with nothing left pointing at either.
      const removal = removeWorktreeDir(wt.repo, wt.path);
      if (removal.action === 'blocked') { report.mergedRemoveFail.push({ name: wt.name, reason: removal.reason }); continue; }
      // LOCAL-ONLY: doctor never touches GitHub (a cron may be pruning a shared machine). A flipped
      // merged worktree has no claims by the flip guard; a ship --keep-worktree residual claim is for
      // an already-shipped (Closes) issue, so we drop it locally and let claims --sync reconcile GitHub.
      state.mutate((st3) => {
        for (const k of Object.keys(st3.claims)) if (st3.claims[k].worktree === wt.name) delete st3.claims[k];
        for (const p of Object.keys(st3.ports)) {
          if (st3.ports[p].owner.type === 'worktree' && st3.ports[p].owner.ref === wt.name) { delete st3.ports[p]; report.prunedPorts.push(Number(p)); }
        }
        delete st3.worktrees[wt.name];
      });
    }
  }

  // 7. shipped-but-undeleted branches. Report-only in every mode (see shippedBranches).
  {
    const st4 = state.loadState();
    const repos = [...new Set([...(config.repos || []), ...knownRepos(config)].map((r) => path.resolve(r)))];
    report.shippedBranches = shippedBranches(repos, st4);
  }

  // 8. ghost ports: something is LISTENING on a port the registry considers free.
  //    Join two books and report the disagreement — never pick a winner, and never kill by port
  //    (only cwd proves ownership; see lib/procs.js). Scoped to the allocation window and minus
  //    the reserved trunk ports, which are SUPPOSED to be bound.
  {
    const st5 = state.loadState();
    const [lo, hi] = ports.parseRange(config.portRange || '5200-5999');
    const reserved = new Set(ports.reservedSet(config, knownRepos(config)).keys());
    const allocated = new Set(Object.keys(st5.ports).map(Number));
    const seen = new Set();
    for (const l of procs.listeners()) {
      if (!(l.port >= lo && l.port <= hi)) continue;
      if (allocated.has(l.port) || reserved.has(l.port) || seen.has(l.port)) continue;
      seen.add(l.port);
      const cwd = procs.cwdOf(l.pid);
      report.ghostPorts.push({ port: l.port, pid: l.pid, command: procs.commandOf(l.pid), cwd, cwdGone: !!cwd && !fs.existsSync(cwd) });
    }
  }

  // 9. spent `group:<key>` labels (#85) — the back-fill for what ship's B4 teardown cannot reach.
  //    B4 (#82) only considers labels carried by the issues the shipping branch just closed, which
  //    is the right trigger going forward but leaves two populations stranded forever: groups whose
  //    members all closed BEFORE that feature existed, and groups closed by any route other than
  //    `ship`. Neither has an unshipped member left to trigger a ship, so nothing else will look.
  //
  //    Behind --sync because it is the ONLY network work doctor does, and doctor is otherwise
  //    instant and fleet-wide — the same opt-in shape `colab claims --sync` already establishes.
  //    Deletion additionally needs --prune, and only ever touches `spent`; see lib/group-labels.js
  //    for why `empty` is reported but never deleted.
  if (opts.sync) {
    if (!git.ghAvailable()) {
      warn('gh not available — --sync group-label check skipped');
      report.groupLabelsChecked = false;
    } else {
      const st6 = state.loadState();
      // Same repo set as pass 7 — the REGISTRY, not just repos with live state. A spent group
      // label is by definition on a repo whose work is finished, so it typically has no worktree
      // and no claim left; deriving repos from state alone would miss exactly the #85 case.
      const repos = [...new Set([...(config.repos || []), ...knownRepos(config)].map((r) => path.resolve(r)))]
        .filter((r) => repoLive(r));
      for (const repo of repos) {
        const verdicts = groupLabels.classifyGroupLabels(
          git.ghListLabels(repo),
          (name) => git.ghIssueListByLabel(repo, name, 'all', ['number', 'state']),
        );
        for (const v of groupLabels.reportableLabels(verdicts)) {
          const entry = { repo: shortRepo(repo), label: v.name, verdict: v.verdict,
            total: v.total, open: v.open, members: v.members, deleted: false, error: null };
          if (opts.prune && v.verdict === 'spent') {
            const del = git.ghLabelDelete(repo, v.name);
            entry.deleted = !!del.ok;
            if (!del.ok) entry.error = del.stderr || 'gh label delete failed';
          }
          report.spentGroupLabels.push(entry);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  if (opts.json) { print(JSON.stringify(report, null, 2)); return 0; }

  const verb = opts.prune ? 'Pruned' : 'Found';
  let issues = 0;
  if (report.deadWorktrees.length) {
    issues += report.deadWorktrees.length;
    print(`${verb} ${report.deadWorktrees.length} dead worktree(s) (directory gone):`);
    for (const w of report.deadWorktrees) print(`  ${w.name} [${w.repo}] claims=${w.claims.join(' ') || '-'} ports=${w.ports.join(',') || '-'}${displayIdentity(w.sessionName, w.session) ? ` session=${displayIdentity(w.sessionName, w.session)}` : ''}`);
  }
  if (report.orphanClaims.length) {
    issues += report.orphanClaims.length;
    print(`${verb} ${report.orphanClaims.length} orphan claim(s) (worktree not in state):`);
    for (const c of report.orphanClaims) print(`  ${c.issue} [${c.repo}] → missing worktree "${c.worktree}"`);
  }
  if (report.staleClaims.length) {
    print(`${opts.prune ? 'Pruned' : 'Flagged'} ${report.staleClaims.length} stale worktree-less claim(s) (> ${ttl}h):`);
    for (const c of report.staleClaims) print(`  ${c.issue} [${c.repo}] age=${c.ageHours}h host=${c.host}${displayIdentity(c.sessionName, c.session) ? ` session=${displayIdentity(c.sessionName, c.session)}` : ''}`);
    if (!opts.prune) print('  (worktree-less claims are not auto-removed — re-run with --prune to remove)');
  }
  if (report.orphanPorts.length) {
    print(`${verb} ${report.orphanPorts.length} orphan port(s):`);
    for (const p of report.orphanPorts) print(`  ${p.port} owner=${p.owner}`);
  }
  if (report.brokenRecords.length) {
    issues += report.brokenRecords.length;
    const healed = report.brokenRecords.filter((r) => r.repaired).length;
    print(`Found ${report.brokenRecords.length} unusable record(s)${healed ? ` (${healed} repaired)` : ''} — a branch or path nothing can resolve:`);
    for (const r of report.brokenRecords) {
      print(`  ${r.kind} ${r.name} [${r.repo}]`);
      for (const p of r.problems) print(`      ${p}`);
      if (r.repaired) print(`      → repaired from disk: branch ${r.repaired.branch} · path ${r.repaired.path}`);
    }
    if (report.brokenRecords.some((r) => !r.repaired)) {
      print('  These fail SILENTLY elsewhere: `landed` cannot classify the worktree, and `ship`');
      print('  matches no claims — so a merge lands with no `Closes #N` and the issue stays open.');
      print('  --prune repairs only what a directory on disk proves; a branch nothing can see is');
      print('  never guessed. Repair the rest through the CLI, never by editing state.json:');
      print('    colab release <N>  &&  colab claim <N> --branch <real-branch> [--worktree <name>]');
      print('    colab worktree rm <name>        # if the worktree is genuinely finished');
    }
  }
  if (report.mergedFlips.length) {
    print(`${opts.prune ? 'Flipped' : 'Detected'} ${report.mergedFlips.length} worktree(s) running → merged (branch landed on trunk, no live claims):`);
    for (const w of report.mergedFlips) print(`  ${w.name} [${w.repo}] branch=${w.branch}`);
    if (!opts.prune) print('  (status recorded on --prune, which also sweeps merged worktrees)');
  }
  if (report.mergedSweep.length) {
    print(`${opts.prune ? 'Swept' : 'Sweep candidates —'} ${report.mergedSweep.length} merged worktree(s) on disk:`);
    for (const w of report.mergedSweep) print(`  ${w.name} [${w.repo}] ports=${w.ports.join(',') || '-'}`);
    if (!opts.prune) print('  (re-run with --prune to tear these down: hook + git worktree remove + free ports)');
  }
  if (report.mergedDirty.length) {
    print(`Skipped ${report.mergedDirty.length} merged worktree(s) holding uncommitted work (not swept):`);
    for (const w of report.mergedDirty) print(`  ${w.name} [${w.repo}] — ${w.what || 'uncommitted work'}; commit it, or remove with: colab worktree rm ${w.name} --force`);
  }
  if (report.mergedHookFail.length) {
    print(`Left ${report.mergedHookFail.length} merged worktree(s) — pre-remove hook vetoed teardown: ${report.mergedHookFail.join(' ')}`);
  }
  if (report.mergedRemoveFail.length) {
    print(`Left ${report.mergedRemoveFail.length} merged worktree(s) — directory removal did not complete (claim(s) NOT released, retry later):`);
    for (const w of report.mergedRemoveFail) print(`  ${w.name} — ${w.reason}`);
  }
  if (opts.prune && report.prunedPorts.length) print(`Freed ports: ${[...new Set(report.prunedPorts)].sort((a, b) => a - b).join(', ')}`);
  // Deliberately NOT part of the health verdict below. `ship` keeps branches by default, so one
  // accumulates per shipped session BY DESIGN — this is a routine maintenance list a human works
  // through when they feel like it, not drift, and not a sign anything went wrong.
  const nothing = issues === 0 && report.staleClaims.length === 0 && report.orphanPorts.length === 0 &&
    report.brokenRecords.length === 0 &&
    report.mergedFlips.length === 0 && report.mergedSweep.length === 0 && report.mergedDirty.length === 0;
  if (nothing) print('All healthy — nothing to prune.');

  // Printed AFTER the health verdict, on purpose: these are not drift. `ship` keeps the branch by
  // default, so this list grows by one per shipped session and is expected to be non-empty.
  if (report.shippedBranches.length) {
    print('');
    print(`Shipped branches you can delete when convenient (${report.shippedBranches.length}) — content is already in trunk:`);
    for (const b of report.shippedBranches) {
      print(`  ${b.branch} [${b.repo}] → in ${b.trunk}${b.remote ? ' · also on origin' : ''}`);
    }
    print('  Routine, not drift: `ship` keeps branches by default, so one lands here per session.');
    print('  Listed here because `git branch --merged` cannot see them — a squash leaves no ancestry.');
    print('  doctor never deletes a ref, --prune included. To remove one:');
    const ex = report.shippedBranches[0];
    print(`    git -C <repo> branch -D ${ex.branch}${ex.remote ? `  &&  git -C <repo> push origin --delete ${ex.branch}` : ''}`);
    print('  NOT exhaustive: a branch whose work trunk later rewrote can conflict against trunk');
    print('  instead of matching it, and is omitted here rather than risk calling unmerged work spent.');
  }
  // Also after the verdict, and also not drift: a spent group label breaks nothing, it is picker
  // noise on a tracker. Printed only under --sync, so silence here NEVER means "checked, clean" —
  // which is why a --sync run that finds nothing says so explicitly below.
  if (report.groupLabelsChecked) {
    const spent = report.spentGroupLabels.filter((g) => g.verdict === 'spent');
    const empty = report.spentGroupLabels.filter((g) => g.verdict === 'empty');
    const unknown = report.spentGroupLabels.filter((g) => g.verdict === 'unknown');
    if (!report.spentGroupLabels.length) {
      print('');
      print('Group labels: checked, none spent — every `group:<key>` still binds an open member.');
    }
    if (spent.length) {
      print('');
      const done = spent.filter((g) => g.deleted).length;
      print(`${opts.prune ? `Deleted ${done}/${spent.length}` : `Spent group labels (${spent.length})`} — every member closed:`);
      for (const g of spent) {
        const marks = g.members.length ? ` (#${g.members.join(' #')})` : '';
        print(`  ${g.label} [${g.repo}] ${g.total} member(s), 0 open${marks}${g.deleted ? '  ✓ deleted' : ''}${g.error ? `  ✗ ${g.error}` : ''}`);
      }
      if (!opts.prune) {
        print('  Routine, not drift: `ship`\'s B4 tears these down going forward, but it can only see');
        print('  labels the shipping branch\'s own issues carried — groups spent before that existed,');
        print('  or closed by any route other than `ship`, have nothing left to trigger a teardown.');
        print('  Re-run with --sync --prune to delete them, or by hand:');
        print(`    gh label delete ${spent[0].label} --yes   # in ${spent[0].repo}`);
      }
    }
    if (empty.length) {
      print('');
      print(`Group labels binding NO issue (${empty.length}) — reported, never auto-deleted:`);
      for (const g of empty) print(`  ${g.label} [${g.repo}]`);
      print('  Two histories produce this and GitHub cannot tell them apart: every member was');
      print('  deleted/transferred (spent), or a triage session created it moments ago and has not');
      print('  applied it yet (LIVE). The labels API exposes no creation time, so --prune leaves');
      print('  these alone and you decide — deleting a group mid-formation is not recoverable in kind.');
    }
    if (unknown.length) {
      print('');
      print(`Group labels that could NOT be checked (${unknown.length}) — membership unreadable:`);
      for (const g of unknown) print(`  ${g.label} [${g.repo}]`);
      print('  Left in place. A failed lookup is not evidence of an empty group.');
    }
  }
  if (report.ghostPorts.length) {
    issues += report.ghostPorts.length;
    print('');
    print(`Ghost ports (${report.ghostPorts.length}) — LISTENING, but the registry says free:`);
    for (const g of report.ghostPorts) {
      print(`  ${g.port}  pid ${g.pid}  ${g.command}`);
      if (g.cwd) print(`        cwd ${g.cwd}${g.cwdGone ? '   ← DELETED (orphan of a torn-down worktree)' : ''}`);
    }
    print('  The next allocation can hand one of these out, and the session then hits EADDRINUSE');
    print('  — or a non-strict dev server slides to another port and the registry is wrong twice.');
    print('  doctor never kills by port (only cwd proves ownership). Inspect, then stop it yourself:');
    print(`    lsof -iTCP:${report.ghostPorts[0].port} -sTCP:LISTEN -P   &&   kill ${report.ghostPorts[0].pid}`);
    print('  A LEGITIMATE long-running service in the window (an emulator, a database) is not drift —');
    print('  declare it so it stops reporting: add its port to `extraReserved` in ~/.colab/config.json,');
    print('  which also stops colab handing it to a worktree. A "cwd DELETED" line above is the real');
    print('  orphan and wants killing, not reserving.');
  }
  return 0;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// release-notes
//
// MIRRORS templates/release-tag.yml "Build grouped release summary" step. The two CANNOT
// share code — the workflow must stay copy-and-own self-contained (git+shell, no Node) — so
// they are deliberate duplicates. If you change the grouping/format here, make the matching
// edit in templates/release-tag.yml (and vice versa). Born from a real gap: when org GitHub
// Actions was billing-locked the workflow couldn't run, and the summary had to be built by
// hand locally. This subcommand makes that manual path first-class and non-drifting.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Conventional Commit types, in display order. Keep in lockstep with the workflow's emit_group
// calls + its Other/Breaking grep alternation.
const CC_TYPES = ['feat', 'fix', 'perf', 'refactor', 'docs', 'chore', 'test'];

const HELP_RELEASE_NOTES = `colab release-notes [<range>] [--repo <path>] [--out <file>] [--headline "<text>"]

Build a grouped Markdown release summary from git history — the same grouping the release
workflow (templates/release-tag.yml) produces, but runnable locally (e.g. when CI is down).

  <range>       a git revision range, e.g. v0.3.0..v0.4.0
                Default: <most recent tag reachable from HEAD>..HEAD
                If the repo has no tags and no range is given, this errors (it will not guess).
  --repo <path> repo root (default: git root of cwd)
  --out <file>  write to a file instead of stdout
  --headline "<text>"   one sentence inserted right after the commit-count line (e.g. the
                        highlight of the release). Optional; nothing is hardcoded.

Output: an H2 "Release summary", a line stating N non-merge commits since <base>, the optional
headline, then "### <type> (<count>)" sections (feat, fix, perf, refactor, docs, chore, test)
with "- <subject>" bullets, plus an "### Other (<count>)" bucket for unprefixed subjects.

Composable — pipe straight into gh:
  colab release-notes v0.3.0..v0.4.0 | gh release create v0.4.0 --notes-file - --generate-notes
`;

/** Left-hand side of a git range as the "base" label, or null if the range isn't a two-dot range. */
function rangeBase(range) {
  const m = String(range).split(/\.\.\.?/); // handles both A..B and A...B
  if (m.length < 2) return null;            // single revision → whole history, no base
  return m[0] || null;                      // guard against "..HEAD"
}

function cmdReleaseNotes(argv) {
  const opts = parseArgs(argv, {
    values: { '--repo': 'repo', '--out': 'out', '--headline': 'headline' },
    flags: { '-h': 'help', '--help': 'help' },
  });
  if (opts.help) return print(HELP_RELEASE_NOTES);
  const repoAbs = resolveRepo(opts);
  if (!git.repoRoot(repoAbs)) throw new UserError(`${repoAbs} is not a git repo`);

  // Resolve the range + its base label.
  let range = opts._[0];
  let base;
  if (range) {
    base = rangeBase(range);
  } else {
    // Default: since the most recent tag reachable from HEAD.
    const d = git.git(['describe', '--tags', '--abbrev=0'], repoAbs);
    if (!d.ok || !d.stdout) {
      throw new UserError('No tags in this repo — pass an explicit <range> (e.g. v0.1.0..HEAD). Refusing to guess.');
    }
    base = d.stdout;
    range = `${base}..HEAD`;
  }

  // Non-merge commit subjects in the range. A merge subject carries no Conventional Commit
  // meaning and would double-count its own commits, so --no-merges (same as the workflow).
  const log = git.git(['log', range, '--no-merges', '--format=%s'], repoAbs);
  if (!log.ok) {
    throw new UserError(`git log ${range} failed: ${log.stderr.split('\n')[0] || `exit ${log.code}`}`);
  }
  const subjects = log.stdout.split('\n').filter(Boolean);

  // Group by type. Match 'feat: x', 'feat(scope): x', and the breaking 'feat!: x' / 'feat(x)!: y'.
  const groups = Object.fromEntries(CC_TYPES.map((t) => [t, []]));
  const other = [];
  const re = new RegExp(`^(${CC_TYPES.join('|')})(\\([^)]*\\))?!?:`);
  for (const s of subjects) {
    const m = s.match(re);
    if (m) groups[m[1]].push(s); else other.push(s);
  }

  // Build Markdown.
  let out = `## Release summary\n\n`;
  out += base
    ? `${subjects.length} commits since \`${base}\`.\n`
    : `${subjects.length} commits.\n`;
  if (opts.headline) out += `\n${opts.headline}\n`;
  out += `\n`;
  for (const t of CC_TYPES) {
    if (!groups[t].length) continue;
    out += `### ${t} (${groups[t].length})\n`;
    out += groups[t].map((s) => `- ${s}`).join('\n') + '\n\n';
  }
  if (other.length) {
    out += `### Other (${other.length})\n`;
    out += other.map((s) => `- ${s}`).join('\n') + '\n\n';
  }

  if (opts.out) {
    fs.writeFileSync(opts.out, out.endsWith('\n') ? out : out + '\n');
    process.stderr.write(`Wrote ${subjects.length} commits (${range}) → ${opts.out}\n`);
  } else {
    process.stdout.write(out);
  }
  return 0;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// release-status — measure merged-but-UNRELEASED lag on tag-gated repos (#81)
//
// CONVENTIONS §6 tells an agent: "if you believe a release is overdue ... say so explicitly in
// your report." Nothing computed whether one IS overdue — this does, read-only. It never promotes,
// never tags, never suggests running a release ritual unattended: the release itself stays a
// human's on every repo, exactly as everywhere else in this CLI (see `promote`, `ship` above).
//
// THE MEASUREMENT GOTCHA THIS IS BUILT AROUND. On a dual-trunk repo (trunk `dev`, promoted into
// `main`) tags live on `main` and are NOT ancestors of the trunk. `git describe` run from the trunk
// checkout returns a STALE tag — the promotion that would have made it current hasn't happened yet,
// by definition, in exactly the case this command exists to measure. Lag is therefore ALWAYS
// computed against `main` (preferring `origin/main`, falling back to the local branch), never
// against the trunk checkout's HEAD, whatever ref happens to be checked out when this runs.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/** `origin/<name>` if it resolves, else the local `<name>`, else null. Never guesses a third ref. */
function bestRef(repoAbs, name) {
  if (git.git(['rev-parse', '--verify', '--quiet', `refs/remotes/origin/${name}`], repoAbs).ok) return `origin/${name}`;
  if (git.git(['rev-parse', '--verify', '--quiet', `refs/heads/${name}`], repoAbs).ok) return name;
  return null;
}

/**
 * Conventional-Commit subjects in <range> (no-merges), classified by CC_TYPES, plus whether any
 * carries a breaking marker — either `!` right before the subject's colon, or a body-level
 * `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer, which a subject-only scan would miss entirely.
 */
function ccSummary(repoAbs, range) {
  const log = git.git(['log', range, '--no-merges', '--format=%s'], repoAbs);
  const subjects = log.ok ? log.stdout.split('\n').filter(Boolean) : [];
  const re = new RegExp(`^(${CC_TYPES.join('|')})(\\([^)]*\\))?(!)?:`);
  const counts = Object.fromEntries(CC_TYPES.map((t) => [t, 0]));
  let breaking = false;
  for (const s of subjects) {
    const m = s.match(re);
    if (m) { counts[m[1]]++; if (m[3]) breaking = true; }
  }
  if (!breaking && log.ok) {
    const bc = git.git(['log', range, '--no-merges', '-i', '-E', '--grep=^BREAKING CHANGE:', '--format=%H'], repoAbs);
    breaking = bc.ok && !!bc.stdout;
  }
  return { total: subjects.length, counts, breaking, subjects };
}

/**
 * feat -> minor, fix -> patch, a breaking change -> major. ADVISORY ONLY: the version number is
 * always the human's — this does arithmetic on Conventional-Commit types, never chooses a release.
 */
function semverSuggestion(cc) {
  if (cc.breaking) return { bump: 'major', why: 'a breaking change (! or BREAKING CHANGE:) since the last tag' };
  if (cc.counts.feat) return { bump: 'minor', why: `${cc.counts.feat} feat commit(s) since the last tag` };
  if (cc.counts.fix) return { bump: 'patch', why: `${cc.counts.fix} fix commit(s) since the last tag` };
  if (cc.total === 0) return { bump: null, why: 'nothing merged since the last tag' };
  return { bump: null, why: 'only non-user-facing commits since the last tag (docs/chore/refactor/test/perf)' };
}

/**
 * One tag-gated repo's release-status row — or an `applicable: false` row with a stated reason
 * (not tag-gated, no working tree, an audit-only slug), the same posture `colab update` uses for a
 * repo it cannot assess. Never throws: a repo this cannot read about is a row, not a crash, because
 * fleet mode must finish the sweep even when one repo is in a bad state.
 */
function inspectReleaseStatus(repoAbs) {
  const label = shortRepo(repoAbs);
  if (!fs.existsSync(repoAbs) || !git.repoRoot(repoAbs)) {
    return { repo: label, path: repoAbs, applicable: false, reason: 'no working tree here' };
  }
  const doc = projectDoc(repoAbs);
  const deploy = doc.deploy ? String(doc.deploy) : '';
  if (deploy !== 'tag') {
    return { repo: label, path: repoAbs, applicable: false, reason: `not tag-gated (deploy: ${deploy || 'absent'})` };
  }
  const trunk = resolveTrunk(repoAbs, doc) || 'main';
  const dualTrunk = trunk !== 'main';

  // Best-effort refresh. A read-only status command must still degrade to whatever is on disk
  // rather than fail the whole row when offline — it says so (`stale: true`) instead of reporting
  // nothing, the same asymmetry `landed.js` and B0's CI re-check apply elsewhere in this file.
  const fetchMain = git.git(['fetch', 'origin', 'main'], repoAbs);
  const fetchTrunk = dualTrunk ? git.git(['fetch', 'origin', trunk], repoAbs) : { ok: true };
  const stale = !fetchMain.ok || !fetchTrunk.ok;

  const mainRef = bestRef(repoAbs, 'main');
  if (!mainRef) return { repo: label, path: repoAbs, applicable: false, reason: 'no "main" ref found locally or on origin — tags are expected to live there' };
  const trunkRef = dualTrunk ? bestRef(repoAbs, trunk) : mainRef;
  if (dualTrunk && !trunkRef) return { repo: label, path: repoAbs, applicable: false, reason: `no "${trunk}" ref found locally or on origin` };

  // unpromoted — dual-trunk only. A single-trunk tag-gated repo (deploy: tag, trunk: main) has
  // nothing that "isn't on main yet" by construction: promotion isn't a step it has.
  let unpromoted = null;
  if (dualTrunk) {
    const cc = ccSummary(repoAbs, `${mainRef}..${trunkRef}`);
    unpromoted = { commits: cc.total, fixFlag: cc.counts.fix > 0 || cc.breaking, subjects: cc.subjects };
  }

  // unreleased — always against main. See the measurement-gotcha note above the section header.
  const describe = git.git(['describe', '--tags', '--abbrev=0', mainRef], repoAbs);
  let unreleased;
  let suggestion = { bump: null, why: 'no tags yet — nothing to suggest against' };
  if (!describe.ok || !describe.stdout) {
    unreleased = { tag: null, commits: null, days: null, fixFlag: false, subjects: [] };
  } else {
    const tag = describe.stdout;
    const cc = ccSummary(repoAbs, `${tag}..${mainRef}`);
    const created = git.git(['for-each-ref', '--format=%(creatordate:iso-strict)', `refs/tags/${tag}`], repoAbs);
    const days = created.ok && created.stdout ? Math.floor((Date.now() - new Date(created.stdout).getTime()) / 86400000) : null;
    unreleased = { tag, commits: cc.total, days, fixFlag: cc.counts.fix > 0 || cc.breaking, subjects: cc.subjects };
    suggestion = semverSuggestion(cc);
  }

  const flag = !!(unreleased.fixFlag || (unpromoted && unpromoted.fixFlag));
  return { repo: label, path: repoAbs, applicable: true, trunk, dualTrunk, stale, unpromoted, unreleased, flag, semverSuggestion: suggestion };
}

const HELP_RELEASE_STATUS = `colab release-status [<repo> ...] [--repo <path>] [--json]

Read-only. Measures merged-but-UNRELEASED lag on tag-gated repos (deploy: tag) — the rung
CONVENTIONS.md §6 asks a human to notice by eye ("if you believe a release is overdue, say so") and
nothing computed until now. NEVER promotes, never tags, never suggests running a release ritual
unattended — the release itself stays a human's on every repo, exactly as \`promote\`/\`ship\` above.

  <repo> ...     limit a fleet sweep the same way \`colab update\` does — an absolute path, or a
                 trailing path segment ("my-repo", "company/project"). Ignored together with --repo.
  --repo <path>  ONE repo only, instead of a fleet sweep.
  --json         machine-readable

FLEET MODE (default, no --repo): sweeps every repo in <COLAB_HOME>/repos.txt — the same registry
\`colab update\` sweeps. A repo whose project.yml isn't \`deploy: tag\` is reported n-a with a reason,
not silently skipped; an owner/name slug (audit-only, no working tree here) is n-a too.

Per tag-gated repo:
  unpromoted        dual-trunk repos only (trunk != main): commits on trunk not yet on main.
  unreleased        commits on main past the last v* tag, plus days since that tag was created.
  flag              true when a fix:-typed (or breaking) commit sits in EITHER gap — the exact class
                     of commit CONVENTIONS §6 already singles out ("an unreleased production fix ...
                     has bitten before").
  semverSuggestion  ADVISORY ONLY, from Conventional-Commit types since the last tag: a breaking
                     change (! or a BREAKING CHANGE: footer) -> major, else feat -> minor, else
                     fix -> patch, else no suggestion. The version number is always the human's.

Each row best-effort fetches \`origin/main\` (and \`origin/<trunk>\` on a dual-trunk repo) first; a
fetch failure does not abort the row — it degrades to whatever is on disk and says so (stale: true)
rather than reporting nothing.

Exit code: 0 when nothing is flagged, 1 when at least one tag-gated repo is (an unpromoted or
unreleased fix/breaking commit) — same posture as \`colab update\`'s "behind" tally.
`;

function cmdReleaseStatus(argv) {
  const opts = parseArgs(argv, {
    values: { '--repo': 'repo' },
    flags: { '--json': 'json', '-h': 'help', '--help': 'help' },
  });
  if (opts.help) return print(HELP_RELEASE_STATUS);

  let rows;
  if (opts.repo) {
    rows = [inspectReleaseStatus(resolveRepo(opts))];
  } else {
    const registryPath = reposTxtPath();
    const entries = reposTxtEntries(readReposTxt());
    if (!entries.length) throw new UserError(`No repos registered (${registryPath}) — add one with: colab register [<path>]`);
    const filters = opts._;
    const selected = filters.length
      ? entries.filter((e) => filters.some((f) => {
        if (e === f || e === path.resolve(f)) return true;
        const norm = e.replace(/\/+$/, '');
        return norm.endsWith(`/${f.replace(/^\/+|\/+$/g, '')}`);
      }))
      : entries;
    if (filters.length && !selected.length) throw new UserError(`No registered repo matches: ${filters.join(', ')} — see \`colab register --list\``);
    rows = selected.map((e) => (isSlug(e)
      ? { repo: e, path: e, applicable: false, reason: 'owner/name slug — audit-only, no working tree here' }
      : inspectReleaseStatus(e)));
  }

  const flagged = rows.filter((r) => r.applicable && r.flag);

  if (opts.json) {
    print(JSON.stringify({
      generated: nowISO(), rows,
      summary: { repos: rows.length, applicable: rows.filter((r) => r.applicable).length, flagged: flagged.length },
    }, null, 2));
    return flagged.length ? 1 : 0;
  }

  const printable = rows.filter((r) => r.applicable);
  const skipped = rows.filter((r) => !r.applicable);
  if (!printable.length) {
    print('No tag-gated repo to report on.');
  } else {
    const tableRows = printable.map((r) => {
      const up = r.unpromoted ? `${r.unpromoted.commits}${r.unpromoted.fixFlag ? ' ⚠' : ''}` : '—';
      const un = r.unreleased.tag ? `${r.unreleased.commits}${r.unreleased.fixFlag ? ' ⚠' : ''} (${r.unreleased.days}d since ${r.unreleased.tag})` : 'no tags yet';
      return [r.repo, up, un, r.semverSuggestion.bump || '—', r.flag ? '⚠ FLAGGED' : ''];
    });
    print(table(tableRows, ['REPO', 'UNPROMOTED', 'UNRELEASED', 'SUGGEST', '']));
  }
  if (skipped.length) {
    print('');
    print(`${skipped.length} repo(s) not assessed:`);
    for (const s of skipped) print(`  ${s.repo}  —  ${s.reason}`);
  }
  const staleRepos = printable.filter((r) => r.stale);
  if (staleRepos.length) {
    print('');
    warn(`${staleRepos.length} repo(s) could not fetch origin — numbers reflect what is on disk, may be stale: ${staleRepos.map((r) => r.repo).join(', ')}`);
  }
  print('');
  if (flagged.length) {
    print(`${flagged.length} repo(s) FLAGGED — a fix or breaking commit is sitting unreleased/unpromoted:`);
    for (const r of flagged) print(`  ${r.repo}`);
    print('Releasing is a human decision on every repo (CONVENTIONS §6) — this only measures the gap.');
  } else {
    print('Nothing flagged.');
  }
  return flagged.length ? 1 : 0;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// register — keep the TWO machine-local registries in sync in one act
//
// The fleet lives in two files that used to be hand-edited separately (the two-places-drift
// disease this handbook fights):
//   1. <COLAB_HOME>/repos.txt      the audit fleet list (audit.mjs reads it; COLAB_HOME-aware)
//   2. <COLAB_HOME>/config.json    repos[] — the reserved-ports aggregation source (this CLI)
// `colab register` writes BOTH, dedups, and `--list` flags any drift between them.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

const REPOS_TXT_HEADER =
`# Fleet registry — MACHINE-LOCAL, not committed. Read by audit.mjs and colab.
# One entry per line: an absolute repo path, or an owner/name slug. '#' starts a comment.
# Managed by \`colab register\` (which keeps this file and config.json repos[] in sync).
`;

function reposTxtPath() { return path.join(state.COLAB_DIR, 'repos.txt'); }

function readReposTxt() {
  try { return fs.readFileSync(reposTxtPath(), 'utf8'); }
  catch (e) { if (e.code === 'ENOENT') return null; throw e; }
}

/** Stripped, non-empty entry values from repos.txt content. */
function reposTxtEntries(text) {
  if (!text) return [];
  return text.split(/\r?\n/).map((l) => l.replace(/#.*$/, '').trim()).filter(Boolean);
}

/** An owner/name slug (audit-only) rather than a local path — can't be a port-scan root. */
function isSlug(x) { return !x.startsWith('/') && !x.startsWith('~') && /^[^/]+\/[^/]+$/.test(x); }

const HELP_REGISTER = `colab register [<path>] [--remove] [--list]

Register a repo into BOTH machine-local registries at once, so they never drift:
  • <COLAB_HOME>/repos.txt   — the audit fleet list (audit.mjs)
  • <COLAB_HOME>/config.json  — repos[], the reserved-ports aggregation source (this CLI)

  <path>      repo to register (default: the git root of the current directory). Must be a
              git repo — non-git paths are refused. The path is normalized to its toplevel.
  --remove    remove the repo from BOTH registries.
  --list      show every registered repo and which registry knows it (T = repos.txt,
              C = config.json). A local path present in only one registry is DRIFT and is
              flagged; slugs are audit-only (repos.txt) and never counted as drift.
              Exits non-zero if any drift is found (scriptable), 0 otherwise.

Idempotent: registering an already-registered repo reports it and exits 0.
`;

function cmdRegister(argv) {
  const opts = parseArgs(argv, {
    flags: { '--remove': 'remove', '--list': 'list', '-h': 'help', '--help': 'help' },
  });
  if (opts.help) return print(HELP_REGISTER);
  if (opts.list) return registerList();

  // Resolve target: explicit path arg, else the current repo root. Refuse non-git.
  let target = opts._[0] ? path.resolve(opts._[0]) : git.repoRoot(process.cwd());
  if (!target) throw new UserError('Not inside a git repo — pass a <path> to register');
  const root = git.repoRoot(target);
  if (!root) throw new UserError(`Not a git repo: ${target} — register only tracks git repos`);
  target = root; // normalize to the worktree toplevel

  return opts.remove ? registerRemove(target) : registerAdd(target);
}

function registerAdd(target) {
  // One lock guards both files so the two registries update together.
  const res = state.withLock(() => {
    fs.mkdirSync(state.COLAB_DIR, { recursive: true });
    // repos.txt
    const text = readReposTxt();
    const inTxt = reposTxtEntries(text).includes(target);
    if (!inTxt) {
      let base;
      if (text == null) base = REPOS_TXT_HEADER + '\n';
      else base = text === '' || text.endsWith('\n') ? text : text + '\n';
      state.atomicWrite(reposTxtPath(), base + target + '\n');
    }
    // config.json repos[]
    const cfg = state.loadConfig();
    const inCfg = cfg.repos.includes(target);
    if (!inCfg) { cfg.repos.push(target); state.saveConfig(cfg); }
    return { inTxt, inCfg };
  });

  if (res.inTxt && res.inCfg) { print(`Already registered in both registries: ${target}`); return 0; }
  const added = [];
  if (!res.inTxt) added.push('repos.txt');
  if (!res.inCfg) added.push('config.json');
  const healed = (res.inTxt || res.inCfg) ? '  (other registry already had it — drift healed)' : '';
  print(`Registered ${target}`);
  print(`  added to: ${added.join(' + ')}${healed}`);
  return 0;
}

function registerRemove(target) {
  const res = state.withLock(() => {
    const text = readReposTxt();
    let removedTxt = false;
    if (text != null) {
      const lines = text.split(/\r?\n/);
      const kept = lines.filter((l) => l.replace(/#.*$/, '').trim() !== target);
      removedTxt = kept.length !== lines.length;
      if (removedTxt) state.atomicWrite(reposTxtPath(), kept.join('\n'));
    }
    const cfg = state.loadConfig();
    const before = cfg.repos.length;
    cfg.repos = cfg.repos.filter((r) => r !== target);
    const removedCfg = cfg.repos.length !== before;
    if (removedCfg) state.saveConfig(cfg);
    return { removedTxt, removedCfg };
  });

  if (!res.removedTxt && !res.removedCfg) { print(`Was not registered in either registry: ${target}`); return 0; }
  const from = [];
  if (res.removedTxt) from.push('repos.txt');
  if (res.removedCfg) from.push('config.json');
  print(`Unregistered ${target}`);
  print(`  removed from: ${from.join(' + ')}`);
  return 0;
}

function registerList() {
  const txtEntries = reposTxtEntries(readReposTxt());
  const cfgRepos = state.loadConfig().repos || [];
  const all = [...new Set([...txtEntries, ...cfgRepos])].sort();
  if (all.length === 0) { print('No repos registered. Add one with: colab register [<path>]'); return 0; }

  let drift = 0;
  const rows = all.map((e) => {
    const inTxt = txtEntries.includes(e);
    const inCfg = cfgRepos.includes(e);
    const slug = isSlug(e);
    let note = '';
    if (slug) note = 'slug — audit-only';
    else if (inTxt && !inCfg) { note = 'DRIFT: only in repos.txt'; drift++; }
    else if (!inTxt && inCfg) { note = 'DRIFT: only in config.json'; drift++; }
    return [inTxt ? 'T' : '-', slug ? 'n/a' : (inCfg ? 'C' : '-'), e, note];
  });
  print(table(rows, ['TXT', 'CFG', 'REPO', 'NOTE']));
  if (drift) {
    print('');
    print(`${drift} repo(s) drifted between the two registries.`);
    print('  Fix: `colab register <path>` re-syncs both, or `colab register <path> --remove` drops from both.');
    return 1;
  }
  return 0;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// config (show / manage the aggregation inputs)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

const HELP_CONFIG = `colab config [show|add-repo <path>|rm-repo <path>|add-reserved-file <path>|rm-reserved-file <path>|set <key> <value>]

  show                       print resolved config, file paths, and the aggregated reserved set
  add-repo <path>            add a repo root to scan for .github/project.yml reserved ports
  rm-repo <path>             remove a repo root
  add-reserved-file <path>   add a machine-local reserved-ports file (~ is expanded); its ports join
                             the reserved set (lenient parse: whitespace ints per line, # comments)
  rm-reserved-file <path>    remove a reserved-ports file
  set <key> <value>          set claimTTLHours | portRange | worktreeSubdir | notifyUrl | journal

notifyUrl is OPTIONAL and off by default: unset, colab makes no network call of its own. Set it to
an http(s) endpoint and claim / release / ship / worktree new / worktree rm each push one small
best-effort JSON event as they succeed — fire-and-forget, never retried, never able to fail or slow
the command that triggered it. Observers already poll this state file; the push only sharpens the
timestamp. Unset it again with:  colab config set notifyUrl ""

journal is OPTIONAL and off by default: unset, colab writes no journal file at all. Set it and every
state transition and every invocation is appended to ~/.colab/journal.jsonl — one JSON object per
line, local, never sent anywhere. It is what lets you ask afterwards how long a worktree lived,
which invocations failed, and where the wall clock went; state.json cannot answer any of those,
because it DELETES records rather than retiring them. Turn it off again with:
  colab config set journal false        (removes the key; the existing file is left alone)
`;

function cmdConfig(argv) {
  const sub = argv[0];
  const cfg = state.loadConfig();
  if (!sub || sub === 'show') {
    print(`state:  ${state.STATE_FILE}`);
    print(`config: ${state.CONFIG_FILE}`);
    print(JSON.stringify(cfg, null, 2));
    const reserved = ports.reservedSet(cfg, knownRepos(cfg));
    print(`reserved ports (${reserved.size}): ${[...reserved.keys()].sort((a, b) => a - b).join(', ') || '—'}`);
    return 0;
  }
  if (sub === 'add-repo') {
    const p = path.resolve(argv[1] || '');
    if (!argv[1]) throw new UserError('add-repo needs a path');
    if (!cfg.repos.includes(p)) cfg.repos.push(p);
    state.saveConfig(cfg); print(`repos: ${cfg.repos.join(', ')}`); return 0;
  }
  if (sub === 'rm-repo') {
    const p = path.resolve(argv[1] || '');
    cfg.repos = cfg.repos.filter((r) => r !== p);
    state.saveConfig(cfg); print(`repos: ${cfg.repos.join(', ') || '(none)'}`); return 0;
  }
  if (sub === 'add-reserved-file') {
    if (!argv[1]) throw new UserError('add-reserved-file needs a path');
    cfg.reservedFiles = cfg.reservedFiles || [];
    // Keep the path as written (may contain ~); expansion happens at read time.
    if (!cfg.reservedFiles.includes(argv[1])) cfg.reservedFiles.push(argv[1]);
    state.saveConfig(cfg);
    const found = ports.parseReservedFile(argv[1]);
    print(`reservedFiles: ${cfg.reservedFiles.join(', ')}`);
    print(`  ${argv[1]} → ${found.length} port(s) parsed`);
    return 0;
  }
  if (sub === 'rm-reserved-file') {
    if (!argv[1]) throw new UserError('rm-reserved-file needs a path');
    cfg.reservedFiles = (cfg.reservedFiles || []).filter((f) => f !== argv[1]);
    state.saveConfig(cfg); print(`reservedFiles: ${cfg.reservedFiles.join(', ') || '(none)'}`); return 0;
  }
  if (sub === 'set') {
    const [k, v] = [argv[1], argv[2]];
    if (!k || v === undefined) throw new UserError('set needs <key> <value>');
    if (k === 'claimTTLHours') cfg[k] = parseFloat(v);
    else if (k === 'portRange' || k === 'worktreeSubdir') cfg[k] = v;
    else if (k === 'journal') {
      // Same "absent, not falsy" discipline as notifyUrl: off is the key not being there, so
      // "turned it off" and "never turned it on" are one state on disk. Only the exact strings
      // below are accepted — a typo must not silently start growing a file nobody asked for.
      if (v === 'false' || v === 'off' || v === '') delete cfg[k];
      else if (v === 'true' || v === 'on') cfg[k] = true;
      else throw new UserError('journal must be true or false');
    }
    else if (k === 'notifyUrl') {
      // Empty string DELETES the key rather than storing '', so "turn it off" and "never turned it
      // on" are the same state on disk. A stored '' would read as a configured-but-broken endpoint.
      if (v === '') delete cfg[k];
      else if (!/^https?:\/\//i.test(v)) throw new UserError('notifyUrl must be an http(s) URL (or "" to unset)');
      else cfg[k] = v;
    }
    else throw new UserError(`Unknown config key "${k}"`);
    state.saveConfig(cfg); print(`${k} = ${cfg[k] === undefined ? '(unset)' : cfg[k]}`); return 0;
  }
  return print(HELP_CONFIG);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// template — copy a handbook workflow template into a repo, STAMPED with the
// handbook's current version. The stamp (`# colab-handbook: <name> @ <version>`) is
// what audit/audit.mjs reads to tell an adopter their copy has fallen behind a changed
// template. Copy-and-own is deliberate: these are never called remotely. Making
// copy+stamp one command matters because a manual stamp is the step people skip.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/** Handbook root + templates dir, relative to this script (tools/colab). */
function handbookPaths() {
  const root = path.resolve(__dirname, '..');
  return { root, templatesDir: path.join(root, 'templates') };
}

/** Where `install.sh --tools` freezes its stamped copy of this CLI (COLAB_HOME-aware). */
function frozenDir() { return path.join(state.COLAB_DIR, 'bin'); }
function frozenBin() { return path.join(frozenDir(), 'colab'); }

/** The stamp sidecar beside a frozen copy, or null. Same convention as a workflow stamp. */
function readFrozenStamp(dir) {
  try {
    return stamp.parseWorkflowStamp(fs.readFileSync(path.join(dir, stamp.FROZEN_STAMP_FILE), 'utf8'));
  } catch (_) {
    return null;
  }
}

/**
 * Which colab is running: the frozen snapshot, or the working tree it was symlinked from?
 *
 * Worth printing rather than assuming, because the two answer differently to the same question by
 * design — the symlinked one follows whatever branch the handbook clone has checked out, the frozen
 * one does not. A service that was meant to be on the frozen path and silently is not looks
 * identical from the outside until a checkout changes its behaviour; `--version` makes it visible.
 */
function selfProvenance() {
  const st = readFrozenStamp(__dirname);
  if (st && st.name === stamp.FROZEN_STAMP_NAME) {
    return { frozen: true, version: st.version, source: __dirname, branch: null };
  }
  const { root } = handbookPaths();
  const hb = stamp.handbookInfo(root);
  const br = stamp.gitIn(root, ['rev-parse', '--abbrev-ref', 'HEAD']);
  return { frozen: false, version: hb.version, source: root, branch: br.ok && br.out ? br.out : null };
}

function cmdVersion() {
  const self = selfProvenance();
  const where = self.frozen
    ? `frozen copy: ${self.source}`
    : `working tree: ${self.source}${self.branch ? `, branch ${self.branch}` : ''}`;
  return print(`colab 1.0.0 — colab-handbook ${self.version} (${where})`);
}

/** Current handbook version = nearest tag; 'v0' before the first tag exists. */
function handbookVersion(root) {
  const d = git.git(['describe', '--tags', '--abbrev=0'], root);
  return d.ok && d.stdout ? d.stdout : 'v0';
}

function listTemplates(templatesDir) {
  try {
    return fs.readdirSync(templatesDir)
      .filter((f) => /\.ya?ml$/.test(f))
      .map((f) => f.replace(/\.ya?ml$/, ''))
      .sort();
  } catch (_) {
    throw new UserError(`Cannot read handbook templates dir: ${templatesDir}`);
  }
}

const HELP_TEMPLATE = `colab template [<name>] [--dest <path>] [--repo <path>] [--force]

Copy a colab-handbook workflow template into a repo, STAMPED with the handbook's current
version. The stamp lets the audit tell you later when the source template has moved on.

  colab template                    list available templates + current handbook version
  colab template <name>             copy templates/<name>.yml → <repo>/.github/workflows/<name>.yml
  colab template <name> --dest F    copy to an explicit file F
                                    (adopters usually want --dest .github/workflows/ci.yml)

Options:
  --repo <path>   target repo root for the default destination (default: git root of cwd)
  --force         overwrite an existing destination. You OWN the copy — local edits are lost.

The copied file gets one line prepended:
  # colab-handbook: <name> @ <version>
These templates are NEVER called remotely — copy, edit, own. When the audit flags your
copy as behind a changed template, review the diff and re-copy to re-stamp.
`;

function cmdTemplate(argv) {
  const opts = parseArgs(argv, {
    values: { '--dest': 'dest', '--repo': 'repo' },
    flags: { '--force': 'force', '-h': 'help', '--help': 'help' },
  });
  if (opts.help) return print(HELP_TEMPLATE);

  const { root, templatesDir } = handbookPaths();
  const available = listTemplates(templatesDir);
  const version = handbookVersion(root);

  // No name → list.
  const name = opts._[0];
  if (!name) {
    print(`colab-handbook templates (handbook ${version}):`);
    for (const t of available) print(`  ${t}`);
    print('');
    print('Copy one:  colab template <name> [--dest <path>] [--repo <path>]');
    print('The CLAUDE block (templates/repo-CLAUDE-block.md) is pasted by hand — see the note in that file.');
    return 0;
  }

  const stem = name.replace(/\.ya?ml$/, '');
  const srcFile = path.join(templatesDir, `${stem}.yml`);
  if (!fs.existsSync(srcFile)) {
    throw new UserError(`No template "${stem}". Available: ${available.join(', ') || '(none)'}`);
  }

  // Destination: explicit --dest, else <repo>/.github/workflows/<name>.yml.
  let destFile;
  if (opts.dest) {
    destFile = path.resolve(opts.dest);
  } else {
    const repoAbs = resolveRepo(opts);
    destFile = path.join(repoAbs, '.github', 'workflows', `${stem}.yml`);
  }

  if (fs.existsSync(destFile) && !opts.force) {
    warn(`${destFile} already exists — not overwriting.`);
    print('Compare your copy against the current template:');
    print(`  diff "${destFile}" "${srcFile}"`);
    print('Re-copy with --force to replace it (you own that file; local edits are lost).');
    return 1;
  }

  if (version === 'v0') {
    warn('handbook has no tags yet — stamping @ v0. Tag the handbook, then re-copy to stamp a real version.');
  }

  const body = fs.readFileSync(srcFile, 'utf8');
  const stampLine = `# colab-handbook: ${stem} @ ${version}\n`;
  fs.mkdirSync(path.dirname(destFile), { recursive: true });
  fs.writeFileSync(destFile, stampLine + body);

  print(`✓ ${stem} @ ${version} → ${destFile}`);
  print('  stamped. Walk its # EDIT: markers, declare your toolchain, then commit. You own this file now.');
  return 0;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// update — sweep the machine's fleet registry and refresh stamped handbook artifacts.
//
// The handbook cannot push to its consumers: the fleet registry is machine-local and
// deliberately uncommitted (this repo is public; a list of private repo paths is not something
// to publish — CONVENTIONS §8). So this is an OUTWARD SWEEP from the machine that holds the
// list, not a broadcast from the handbook.
//
// Three refusals define this command, and each one protects something specific:
//
//   1. REPORT BY DEFAULT, write only under --apply. Copy-and-own (§7) means a repo may
//      legitimately hand-edit its CI; a tool that silently overwrote it would destroy the very
//      principle §7 exists to serve.
//   2. NEVER COMMIT, STAGE OR PUSH. Every repo has its own tier and trunk rules — committing
//      into a Tier A repo's `dev` would have this tool violate the handbook it enforces. It
//      writes files into the working tree and stops; the human commits through that repo's flow.
//   3. NEVER TOUCH A DIVERGED COPY, even with --apply. "Diverged" means someone edited it, and
//      we cannot know what their edit was for.
//
// The classification (see classifyStamped in lib/stamp.js) rests on two git reads against the
// handbook's own history, NOT on comparing version strings: `git log <stamp>..HEAD` scoped to the
// template answers "did this actually change?", and `git show <stamp>:templates/<name>` answers
// "was this copy pristine when it was made?". Comparing versions alone would mark the whole fleet
// stale on every release, including releases that touched nothing anyone copied.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/** Repo-relative candidates for the CLAUDE conventions block. */
const CLAUDE_FILE = 'CLAUDE.md';

/**
 * Inspect one registry entry. Returns { label, path, kind, artifacts[], note }.
 * `artifacts` entries: { file, template, state, from, to, reason, applicable }.
 */
function inspectRepo(entry, ctx) {
  const label = entryLabel(entry);

  if (isSlug(entry)) {
    return { label, path: null, kind: 'slug', artifacts: [],
      note: 'owner/name slug — audit-only, no working tree on this machine to update' };
  }
  const abs = path.resolve(entry.startsWith('~') ? path.join(os.homedir(), entry.slice(1)) : entry);
  if (!fs.existsSync(abs)) {
    return { label, path: abs, kind: 'missing', artifacts: [], note: 'path does not exist on this machine' };
  }
  // Skip self. A stamp means "copied from version X"; this repo IS X, and its own ci.yml is
  // purpose-written rather than copied — treating it as an adopter is a guaranteed false positive.
  // Identity is by git common dir, not by path string: this CLI resolves its root from its own
  // file, so running it out of a worktree gives a different spelling of the same repo than the
  // fleet registry holds. The string test missed that and reported the handbook as its own adopter.
  if (stamp.isHandbookItself(abs, ctx.handbookRoot)) {
    return { label, path: abs, kind: 'handbook', artifacts: [], note: 'the handbook itself — skipped' };
  }

  const artifacts = [];
  const read = (rel) => {
    const f = path.join(abs, rel);
    try { return fs.existsSync(f) ? fs.readFileSync(f, 'utf8') : null; } catch (_) { return null; }
  };

  // --- workflow copies: whole-file copies, so a pristine one can be regenerated exactly ---
  const wfDir = path.join(abs, '.github', 'workflows');
  let wfs = [];
  try { wfs = fs.existsSync(wfDir) ? fs.readdirSync(wfDir).filter((f) => /\.ya?ml$/.test(f)).sort() : []; }
  catch (_) { wfs = []; }

  for (const wf of wfs) {
    const rel = `.github/workflows/${wf}`;
    const text = read(rel);
    const st = stamp.parseWorkflowStamp(text);
    if (st) {
      const c = stamp.classifyStamped({
        root: ctx.handbookRoot, hb: ctx.hb, tmplNames: ctx.tmplNames,
        templateName: st.name, stampVersion: st.version, localText: text, comparable: true,
      });
      artifacts.push({ file: rel, template: st.name, applicable: c.state === 'behind', ...c });
    } else {
      // No stamp: ask what the CONTENT says, not what the filename says. `unstamped` claims a
      // handbook lineage and points at a real template; `unrelated` is the file whose name
      // collides with a template it never came from — reported here precisely BECAUSE this is
      // the command whose --force would overwrite it. Anything else stays silent: somebody's
      // own workflow is none of our business.
      const prov = stamp.workflowProvenance(text, wf.replace(/\.ya?ml$/, ''), ctx.tmplNames);
      const finding = stamp.unstampedFinding(prov);
      if (finding) {
        artifacts.push({ file: rel, template: prov.template, state: finding.state,
          from: null, to: ctx.hb.version, applicable: false, reason: finding.reason });
      }
    }
  }

  // --- the CLAUDE conventions block ---
  //
  // REPORTED, NEVER WRITTEN — and this is a deliberate departure from treating every stamped
  // artifact alike. The block is a FRAGMENT pasted into a larger hand-written file, and the
  // template ships PLACEHOLDERS the adopter fills in (`<A|B>`, `<dev|main>`). So a correctly
  // adopted block never matches the template byte-for-byte: divergence is undecidable here, and
  // regenerating it would overwrite a repo's real tier/trunk with angle brackets — and take the
  // rest of its CLAUDE.md with it. We classify it (current/behind) and hand it to a human.
  const claude = read(CLAUDE_FILE);
  if (claude) {
    const st = stamp.parseClaudeStamp(claude);
    if (st) {
      const c = stamp.classifyStamped({
        root: ctx.handbookRoot, hb: ctx.hb, tmplNames: ctx.tmplNames,
        templateName: null, stampVersion: st.version, localText: claude, comparable: false,
      });
      artifacts.push({
        file: CLAUDE_FILE, template: 'repo-CLAUDE-block', applicable: false, ...c,
        reason: c.state === 'behind'
          ? 'the conventions block moved — re-paste templates/repo-CLAUDE-block.md by hand and bump its stamp (it has placeholders only you can fill, so this is never automated)'
          : c.reason,
      });
    } else if (stamp.looksLikeHandbookClaude(claude)) {
      artifacts.push({ file: CLAUDE_FILE, template: 'repo-CLAUDE-block', state: 'unstamped',
        from: null, to: ctx.hb.version, applicable: false,
        reason: 'has the conventions block but no stamp — re-paste the current block including its `<!-- colab-handbook @ ... -->` line' });
    }
  }

  const note = artifacts.length ? null : 'no stamped handbook artifacts — nothing adopted here yet';
  return { label, path: abs, kind: 'local', artifacts, note };
}

/**
 * Assess this machine's frozen CLI copy — reported alongside the fleet because it is the same
 * question ("is a stamped copy behind the handbook?") about the one artifact that is NOT in any
 * repo. It is also the one whose staleness nobody would otherwise notice: a service keeps running
 * the old CLI perfectly happily, which is exactly what it was frozen to do.
 *
 * Reported only, never written, even with --apply: refreshing it re-copies the whole toolchain a
 * live service is currently executing. That is install.sh's job and a human's decision.
 */
function inspectFrozen(ctx) {
  const bin = frozenBin();
  if (!fs.existsSync(bin)) {
    return {
      path: bin, installed: false, state: 'absent', from: null, to: ctx.hb.version,
      reason: 'no frozen copy on this machine — an always-on service would have to resolve the CLI through a working tree, and a checkout would then change its behaviour. Install one: ./install.sh --tools',
    };
  }
  const st = readFrozenStamp(frozenDir());
  const c = stamp.classifyFrozen({
    root: ctx.handbookRoot, hb: ctx.hb, stampVersion: st ? st.version : null,
  });
  return { path: bin, installed: true, ...c };
}

/** Two path segments read better than one in a report: `company/project`. */
function entryLabel(entry) {
  if (isSlug(entry)) return entry;
  const r = entry.replace(/\/+$/, '');
  const parent = path.basename(path.dirname(r));
  return parent && parent !== '/' && parent !== '.' ? `${parent}/${path.basename(r)}` : path.basename(r);
}

/** Rewrite a behind-but-pristine workflow copy as the current template, freshly stamped. */
function refreshArtifact(repo, art, ctx) {
  const src = path.join(ctx.templatesDir, `${art.template}.yml`);
  if (!fs.existsSync(src)) return { ok: false, err: `template ${art.template}.yml is not in the handbook` };
  const body = fs.readFileSync(src, 'utf8');
  const dest = path.join(repo.path, art.file);
  try {
    fs.mkdirSync(path.dirname(dest), { recursive: true });
    fs.writeFileSync(dest, stamp.stampLine(art.template, ctx.hb.version) + body);
    return { ok: true };
  } catch (e) {
    return { ok: false, err: e.message };
  }
}

const HELP_UPDATE = `colab update [<repo> ...] [--apply] [--json] [--quiet]

Sweep this machine's fleet registry and report which repos' stamped handbook artifacts have
fallen behind — then, with --apply, refresh the ones that are safe to refresh.

  <repo> ...   limit the sweep (match an absolute path, or a trailing path segment such as
               "my-repo" or "company/project"). Default: every registered repo.
  --apply      WRITE the refreshable copies. Without it nothing on disk is touched.
  --quiet, -q  only show repos with something to say
  --json       machine-readable

Each stamped artifact is classified:
  current     the stamp is the handbook's version, or the template genuinely has not changed
              since that stamp (checked with git log, not by comparing version strings — so a
              release that touched nothing you copied does not mark you stale)
  behind      the stamp is older, the template really did change, AND your copy is still
              pristine as of its own stamp -> safe to regenerate. Only these are written.
  diverged    your copy does not match the template as of ITS OWN stamp: it was hand-edited.
              Copy-and-own makes that legitimate, so it is REPORTED AND NEVER WRITTEN.
  unstamped   the CONTENT matches text only our templates contain, but there is no stamp:
              lineage unknown, so replacing it could destroy edits nobody can see. The row
              names the template the evidence points to — re-copy that one, deliberately.
  unrelated   its NAME matches a template but its content carries nothing of that template.
              A shared filename is a coincidence, not a lineage: this file is the repo's own
              and must NOT be re-copied over. Reported so nobody --forces it by mistake.
  n-a         cannot be assessed, always with a stated reason (an owner/name slug has no
              working tree here; a stamp from a tag this checkout lacks; an unknown template).

It also reports THIS MACHINE's frozen CLI copy (<COLAB_HOME>/bin/colab, written by
./install.sh --tools), classified the same way: current when the CLI has not changed since its
stamp, behind when it has. Always-on services call that copy precisely so a checkout cannot move
them, which means nothing will ever tell you it is old unless something asks — this is that
something. It is REPORTED, NEVER WRITTEN, even with --apply: re-freezing swaps the toolchain a
live service is executing, so it stays a deliberate act (re-run install.sh).

What this command will NOT do, by design:
  • It never commits, stages or pushes. Every repo has its own tier and trunk rules; committing
    for you would make this tool violate the handbook it enforces. It writes files and stops —
    review with \`git diff\` and commit through that repo's own flow.
  • It never rewrites a diverged copy, even with --apply.
  • It never rewrites the CLAUDE conventions block: that block has placeholders only you can
    fill, so it is reported for a hand re-paste.
  • It skips the handbook repo itself.

Exit code: 0 when nothing is behind, 1 when anything is behind — the frozen copy included,
since a stale CLI is as much a thing to act on as a stale workflow. 2 on usage error.
`;

function cmdUpdate(argv) {
  const opts = parseArgs(argv, {
    flags: { '--apply': 'apply', '--json': 'json', '--quiet': 'quiet', '-q': 'quiet', '-h': 'help', '--help': 'help' },
  });
  if (opts.help) return print(HELP_UPDATE);

  // Run from the frozen copy this command has no handbook history to compare anything against —
  // its "root" is <COLAB_HOME>, which is not a git repo. Untagged-handbook output would technically
  // be honest and completely misleading: it reads as "the handbook has no tags".
  const self = selfProvenance();
  if (self.frozen) {
    throw new UserError(
      `this is the frozen copy (${self.source}), which carries no handbook history to compare against.\n` +
      '  Run `colab update` from the handbook working tree — the frozen copy is for services, not for sweeps.');
  }

  const { root, templatesDir } = handbookPaths();
  const hb = stamp.handbookInfo(root);
  const ctx = { handbookRoot: root, templatesDir, hb, tmplNames: stamp.templateNames(root) };
  const frozen = inspectFrozen(ctx);

  const registryPath = reposTxtPath();
  const entries = reposTxtEntries(readReposTxt());
  if (!entries.length) {
    throw new UserError(`No repos registered (${registryPath}) — add one with: colab register [<path>]`);
  }

  // Positional filters: an absolute path, or any trailing path segment ("my-repo").
  const filters = opts._;
  const selected = filters.length
    ? entries.filter((e) => filters.some((f) => {
      if (e === f || e === path.resolve(f)) return true;
      const norm = e.replace(/\/+$/, '');
      return norm.endsWith(`/${f.replace(/^\/+|\/+$/g, '')}`);
    }))
    : entries;
  if (filters.length && !selected.length) {
    throw new UserError(`No registered repo matches: ${filters.join(', ')} — see \`colab register --list\``);
  }

  const repos = selected.map((e) => inspectRepo(e, ctx));

  // --- apply -------------------------------------------------------------
  // Only `behind` + `applicable` artifacts are eligible; everything else was already excluded by
  // the classification, which is where the refusals live.
  const written = [];
  const failed = [];
  if (opts.apply && !hb.untagged) {
    for (const r of repos) {
      for (const a of r.artifacts) {
        if (a.state !== 'behind' || !a.applicable) continue;
        const res = refreshArtifact(r, a, ctx);
        if (res.ok) { a.written = true; written.push({ repo: r.label, file: a.file, template: a.template, from: a.from, to: a.to }); }
        else { a.writeError = res.err; failed.push({ repo: r.label, file: a.file, err: res.err }); }
      }
    }
  }

  // --- tally -------------------------------------------------------------
  const all = repos.flatMap((r) => r.artifacts);
  const count = (s) => all.filter((a) => a.state === s).length;
  const summary = {
    repos: repos.length,
    artifacts: all.length,
    current: count('current'), behind: count('behind'), diverged: count('diverged'),
    unstamped: count('unstamped'), unrelated: count('unrelated'), na: count('n-a'),
    // Repos with no assessable artifact at all are counted SEPARATELY from artifact-level n-a:
    // they are repos, not artifacts, and folding them into the artifact tally would make the
    // arithmetic on that line stop adding up.
    skippedRepos: repos.filter((r) => !r.artifacts.length).length,
    refreshable: all.filter((a) => a.state === 'behind' && a.applicable && !a.written).length,
    written: written.length,
  };

  if (opts.json) {
    print(JSON.stringify({
      generated: nowISO(),
      handbook: { version: hb.version, untagged: hb.untagged, root },
      registry: registryPath,
      applied: !!opts.apply,
      repos, written, failed, frozen, summary,
    }, null, 2));
    return summary.behind > 0 || frozen.state === 'behind' ? 1 : 0;
  }

  // --- report ------------------------------------------------------------
  print(`registry:  ${registryPath}`);
  print(`handbook:  ${hb.version}${hb.untagged ? ' (untagged — stamp comparison inactive)' : ''}`);
  // The frozen CLI first: it is one line, it is about THIS machine rather than the fleet, and it
  // is the only row here that a reader cannot discover any other way.
  if (!(opts.quiet && frozen.state === 'current')) {
    const move = frozen.from ? `${frozen.from} → ${frozen.to}  ` : '';
    print(`frozen:    ${frozen.state}  ${move}${frozen.reason}`);
    if (frozen.installed) print(`           ${frozen.path}`);
  }
  print('');
  if (hb.untagged) {
    warn('the handbook has no tags — there is no version line to compare against. Tag it, then re-run.');
    return 0;
  }

  const rows = [];
  for (const r of repos) {
    if (!r.artifacts.length) {
      if (!opts.quiet) rows.push([r.label, '—', 'n-a', r.note || 'nothing to assess']);
      continue;
    }
    for (const a of r.artifacts) {
      // `unrelated` is a "do not touch this" note, not a finding — it has no action, so it stays
      // out of the quiet view alongside `current`.
      if (opts.quiet && (a.state === 'current' || a.state === 'unrelated')) continue;
      const move = a.from ? `${a.from} → ${a.to}` : `→ ${a.to}`;
      const mark = a.written ? 'updated' : a.state;
      const detail = a.writeError ? `WRITE FAILED: ${a.writeError}` : `${move}  ${a.reason}`;
      rows.push([r.label, a.file, mark, detail]);
    }
  }
  if (rows.length) print(table(rows, ['REPO', 'ARTIFACT', 'STATE', 'DETAIL']));
  else print('Nothing to report — every stamped artifact is current.');

  print('');
  print(`${summary.repos} repo(s), ${summary.artifacts} handbook artifact(s): ` +
    `${summary.current} current, ${summary.behind} behind, ${summary.diverged} diverged, ` +
    `${summary.unstamped} unstamped, ${summary.na} n-a` +
    (summary.unrelated ? `, ${summary.unrelated} unrelated (name clash only — not ours).` : '.'));
  if (summary.skippedRepos) {
    print(`${summary.skippedRepos} repo(s) had nothing to assess (nothing adopted, an audit-only slug, a missing path, or the handbook itself).`);
  }

  if (opts.apply) {
    if (written.length) {
      print(`Refreshed ${written.length} file(s) to ${hb.version}:`);
      for (const w of written) print(`  ✓ ${w.repo}  ${w.file}  (${w.template} ${w.from} → ${w.to})`);
      print('');
      print('NOTHING was committed, staged or pushed — that is deliberate: each repo has its own');
      print('tier and trunk rules. Review with `git diff` and commit through that repo\'s own flow.');
    } else {
      print('Nothing was refreshable — no files were written.');
    }
    for (const f of failed) warn(`could not write ${f.repo} ${f.file}: ${f.err}`);
  } else if (summary.refreshable) {
    print(`${summary.refreshable} pristine copy/copies can be refreshed: re-run with --apply.`);
  }

  if (summary.diverged || summary.unstamped) {
    print(`${summary.diverged} diverged + ${summary.unstamped} unstamped need a human — never written by this command.`);
    print('  Review the diff, take what you want, then re-copy the template NAMED IN THAT ROW.');
    print('  Where a row names none, its lineage is unproven — establish it before stamping one.');
  }
  if (summary.unrelated) {
    print(`${summary.unrelated} unrelated file(s) share a template's name without its content — leave them alone.`);
  }
  if (frozen.state === 'behind') {
    print('');
    print(`The frozen CLI at ${frozen.path} is ${frozen.from}, the handbook is ${frozen.to}.`);
    print('  Re-freeze deliberately, from the handbook clone:  ./install.sh --tools');
    print('  --apply never touches it: it is the toolchain your always-on services are running.');
  }

  return summary.behind > 0 || frozen.state === 'behind' ? 1 : 0;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ship — the ONE sanctioned door for code-wrap Phase B (session branch → the base it was cut from).
//
// Autonomy is granted by the REPO (project.yml `autonomy: auto-trunk`), never by the caller: a
// human decides which repos an agent may auto-merge, and --force cannot override that. ship never
// touches main (when trunk != main), never tags, never promotes — those stay human. Every step is
// gated; any failure aborts cleanly BEFORE the push, so the target is never left half-shipped.
//
// The target is the worktree's RECORDED BASE, which is trunk unless the session was cut from a
// declared integration line. Following the base rather than resolving trunk afresh is not a
// convenience: shipping a line-based branch into trunk would carry the whole line along inside one
// squash commit. Merging a line itself into trunk is refused here in every configuration.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Files that are regenerated, not authored — a merge conflict here is resolvable by re-running the
// repo's codegen, so it doesn't force a human. Repos extend this via project.yml `generated: [...]`.
const DEFAULT_GENERATED = [
  'package-lock.json', 'pnpm-lock.yaml', 'yarn.lock', 'composer.lock', 'Cargo.lock', 'go.sum',
  'dist/', 'build/', 'public/build/', '.astro/',
];

function escapeRe(s) { return s.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\]\\]/g, '\\$&'); }

/** Does a path look generated per the pattern list? `foo/` = prefix, `*` = wildcard, else exact/basename. */
function isGeneratedPath(file, patterns) {
  const base = file.split('/').pop();
  for (const pat of patterns) {
    if (pat.endsWith('/')) { if (file === pat.slice(0, -1) || file.startsWith(pat) || file.includes('/' + pat)) return true; }
    else if (pat.includes('*')) {
      const re = new RegExp('^' + pat.split('*').map(escapeRe).join('.*') + '$');
      if (re.test(file) || re.test(base)) return true;
    } else if (file === pat || base === pat) return true;
  }
  return false;
}

/** Parsed .github/project.yml doc, or {} if absent/unparseable. */
function projectDoc(repoAbs) {
  try { return require('./lib/yaml').parse(fs.readFileSync(path.join(repoAbs, '.github', 'project.yml'), 'utf8')) || {}; }
  catch (_) { return {}; }
}

/** Trunk for a repo: project.yml `trunk:` → origin/HEAD. */
function resolveTrunk(repoAbs, doc) {
  if (doc && doc.trunk) return String(doc.trunk);
  return git.detectTrunk(repoAbs);
}

/**
 * Declared long-lived integration lines (project.yml `integration:`), or [].
 *
 * These are DEVELOPMENT integration points: a worktree may be cut from one and shipped back into
 * one. Nothing in promote / tag / deploy reads this — that is the field's whole guarantee, so keep
 * every use of it on the ship/worktree side of the CLI (project.schema.md).
 *
 * Entries equal to trunk or to `main` are dropped rather than honoured: the audit reports them as
 * findings, and a malformed descriptor must not be the thing that hands a session a base with a
 * path to production. Fail closed, report loudly elsewhere.
 */
function resolveIntegration(repoAbs, doc, trunk) {
  const raw = doc && doc.integration;
  if (!Array.isArray(raw)) return [];
  return raw
    .map((b) => (b === null || b === undefined ? '' : String(b).trim()))
    .filter((b) => b && b !== trunk && b !== 'main' && b !== 'trunk');
}

/** The set of refs a worktree may legitimately be based on: trunk ∪ integration. */
function allowedBases(repoAbs, doc, trunk) {
  return [trunk, ...resolveIntegration(repoAbs, doc, trunk)].filter(Boolean);
}

/**
 * Declared external-deploy release branch (project.yml `releaseBranch:`), or ''.
 *
 * Names a long-lived branch that is NOT trunk and NOT `main`: the extra branch a
 * single-trunk, tag-gated Tier A repo fast-forwards on release for an external GitOps
 * poller to consume (project.schema.md#releasebranch--optional). It is a PRODUCTION ref
 * — the opposite axis from `integration:` — so it is never added to allowedBases() and a
 * worktree may never be cut from or shipped into it.
 *
 * Between releases this branch is, by construction, an ancestor of trunk (it was
 * fast-forwarded to trunk's tip at the last tag, and trunk has since moved on). That is
 * exactly the shape shippedBranches() flags as "already merged, safe to delete" — so this
 * resolver exists to let that check name the one branch where the heuristic is wrong.
 *
 * Same fail-closed rule as resolveIntegration: a value equal to trunk, `main` or the role
 * word `trunk` is dropped rather than honoured — the audit reports it as a finding, and a
 * malformed descriptor must not be the thing that un-protects a real branch by accident.
 */
function resolveReleaseBranch(repoAbs, doc, trunk) {
  const raw = doc && doc.releaseBranch;
  if (raw === null || raw === undefined) return '';
  const b = String(raw).trim();
  return b && b !== trunk && b !== 'main' && b !== 'trunk' ? b : '';
}

/**
 * The base a session branch was cut from, and therefore the branch it ships back into.
 *
 * Recorded per worktree at creation (`state.worktrees[name].base`) because it is a per-machine,
 * per-session fact — the repo declares which bases are LEGAL, the state records which one was
 * USED. Worktrees created before this existed have no `base`, so trunk is the fallback: that is
 * what they were in fact cut from.
 *
 * Base and ship target are ONE decision, not two. A worktree cut from a long-lived line and shipped
 * into trunk would drag the whole line in behind it as a single squash commit that reads like a
 * small change — which is exactly the accident `integration:` exists to make impossible.
 */
function resolveBase(st, repoAbs, worktreeName, trunk) {
  const wt = worktreeName ? st.worktrees[worktreeName] : null;
  const recorded = wt && wt.base ? String(wt.base) : '';
  return recorded || trunk;
}

/**
 * Is <branch>'s content already in the branch it would ship into? A thin adapter over the shared
 * rule in lib/landed.js — see that file for why "commits ahead" is the wrong question, why
 * `git cherry` does not fix it, and exactly what the rule does not cover.
 *
 * `base` is the branch's recorded base when the caller knows it, trunk otherwise. Asking a
 * line-based branch about trunk would report a fully-shipped branch as enormous cargo.
 *
 * Returns { contained, ambiguous, verdict } — `ambiguous` covers missing refs, a conflicting
 * rewrite, and a git too old for the content test. Callers must treat it as NOT contained: this
 * decides whether a worktree is a teardown candidate, and being wrong toward "contained" is the
 * direction that loses work.
 *
 * (The previous implementation read `merge-tree` exit code 1 as "a clean merge with changes". It is
 * a CONFLICT — a clean merge with changes also exits 0, with a differing tree — so a branch whose
 * work trunk had rewritten was reported as a confident "not contained" rather than an honest
 * "cannot tell". Same action, worse information.)
 */
function branchContainedInTrunk(repoAbs, branch, base) {
  const target = base || resolveTrunk(repoAbs, projectDoc(repoAbs));
  if (!target || target === branch) return { contained: false, ambiguous: true };
  const verdict = landed.landedState(repoAbs, target, branch);
  return { contained: verdict.state === 'landed', ambiguous: verdict.state === 'unknown', verdict };
}

/** New migration files introduced on <branch> vs <trunk> (Laravel + Prisma layouts). */
function newMigrations(mainRoot, trunk, branch) {
  const r = git.git(['diff', '--name-only', `${trunk}...${branch}`], mainRoot);
  if (!r.ok) return [];
  return r.stdout.split('\n').filter(Boolean)
    .filter((f) => /(^|\/)database\/migrations\//.test(f) || /(^|\/)prisma\/migrations\//.test(f));
}

/**
 * Migration-grant verdict for a ship's whole claimed issue set (#98) — read from the tracker,
 * validated by tools/lib/migration-grant.js's evaluateShipSet. ONLY called when `newMigrations()`
 * already found at least one migration file: on the ordinary no-migration ship this costs zero
 * extra `gh` reads and the ✓ row stays byte-identical to before this feature existed.
 *
 * Validates over `issues` — the branch's FULL claimed set, not `closeIssues` — deliberately: a
 * group branch carries several issues and a migration cannot be mechanically attributed to one of
 * them (#98 requirement 5), and `closeIssues` is *narrowed* by `--refs`, which would otherwise let
 * a caller-supplied flag shrink a safety gate — the one thing `ship` refuses everywhere else.
 */
function shipMigrationGrants(repoAbs, gh, issues, branch) {
  if (!gh) {
    return { ok: false, granted: [],
      missing: [{ issue: null, reason: 'gh not usable (no auth / no origin) — a migration grant lives on the tracker and cannot be confirmed' }] };
  }
  const records = {};
  for (const n of issues) records[n] = git.ghIssueView(repoAbs, n, ['state', 'labels', 'comments']);
  return migrationGrant.evaluateShipSet(issues, records, branch, labels.MIGRATION_GRANT_LABEL);
}

/**
 * The `no new migrations` row's detail string, and the migrationGrant JSON payload for --dry
 * --json — one function so the two paths (prose table + JSON) can never read differently. `grants`
 * is null when there are no migrations (the ordinary case; grants is never computed then).
 */
function migDetail(migrations, grants, target, branch) {
  if (migrations.length === 0) return { text: `none vs ${target}`, payload: null };
  const files = migrations.join(', ');
  const payload = {
    branch, files: [...migrations],
    granted: grants.granted.map((g) => ({ issue: g.issue, by: g.by, at: g.at })),
    missing: grants.missing.map((m) => ({ issue: m.issue, reason: m.reason })),
  };
  if (grants.ok) {
    const by = grants.granted.map((g) => '#' + g.issue).join(' ');
    return { text: `${migrations.length} present, exempted by a human grant on ${by} for branch "${branch}" — ${files}`, payload };
  }
  // Each missing entry already carries its own specific, actionable reason (evaluateShipSet) —
  // report those verbatim rather than just an issue tag, so an operator or a scheduled driver's
  // park-and-say-once log does not have to go re-read comments to learn WHY. `issue: null` covers
  // two distinct causes (a failed gh read of the whole set, or zero claimed issues to grant at
  // all) and both already say so in their own `reason` string — never paper over either with a
  // placeholder like "(unreadable)" that would misname the zero-issues case.
  const reasons = grants.missing.map((m) => m.reason).join('; ');
  return { text: `${migrations.length} present, human must run Phase B: ${files} — ${reasons}`, payload };
}

/**
 * Read commits the branch itself contributed (newest-first) as {subject, body} records.
 *
 * Split out from message composition on purpose: this half touches git, the other half is pure and
 * lives in lib/squash.js where CI can unit-test it. `tools/colab` has no test harness, which is how
 * the "newest commit titles the squash" bug survived long enough to reach a published tag.
 *
 * TWO THINGS THIS MUST NOT DO, both found live (#57):
 *
 * 1. Walk `--first-parent` from the merge-base, not the full ancestry of `<trunk>..<branch>`. A
 *    plain ancestry diff cannot tell "this session wrote it" from "this session merged it in" — a
 *    commit pulled in from an unrelated branch via `git merge` sits in the same range as the
 *    branch's own work and, if it carries a higher-weight prefix, silently wins the subject. The
 *    first-parent chain is the cheap approximation that excludes it: only commits reached by
 *    walking the branch's own sequence of commits count, not ones that arrived as a merge's other
 *    side.
 * 2. Never substitute the branch's TIP for an empty result. The previous code did exactly that —
 *    `shas.length ? shas : [rev-parse branch]` — reasoning that an empty `git log` meant something
 *    had gone wrong and a fallback commit was safer than none. But an empty range is not always a
 *    failure: a freshly-cut branch with no commits of its own produces one too, correctly, and the
 *    "fallback" then grabs whatever commit sits at the branch's tip — which, having no commits of
 *    its own, IS the base's tip: a commit some other, earlier session wrote. Observed live wrapping
 *    the record-validation work, where a freshly-cut branch dry-ran with a squash subject borrowed
 *    from its base. An empty result is now left empty; the caller decides what to say about it
 *    (buildSquashMessage below), rather than this layer inventing a commit to hide it.
 */
function branchCommits(mainRoot, trunk, branch) {
  const mb = git.git(['merge-base', trunk, branch], mainRoot);
  const base = mb.ok && mb.stdout ? mb.stdout : trunk;
  const logShas = git.git(['log', '--first-parent', `${base}..${branch}`, '--no-merges', '--format=%H'], mainRoot);
  const shas = logShas.ok ? logShas.stdout.split('\n').filter(Boolean) : [];
  return shas.map((sha) => {
    const full = git.git(['log', '-1', '--format=%B', sha], mainRoot).stdout;
    const nl = full.indexOf('\n');
    return {
      sha,
      subject: (nl === -1 ? full : full.slice(0, nl)).replace(/\s+$/, ''),
      body: (nl === -1 ? '' : full.slice(nl + 1)).replace(/^\n+/, '').replace(/\s+$/, ''),
    };
  });
}

/** Files one commit touched (#88's narrow-subject signal). [] when the read fails — never a guess. */
function commitFiles(mainRoot, sha) {
  const r = git.git(['show', '--pretty=format:', '--name-only', sha], mainRoot);
  return r.ok ? [...new Set(r.stdout.split('\n').map((s) => s.trim()).filter(Boolean))] : [];
}

/** Files the whole branch changed against its base — the denominator for the same signal. */
function branchChangedFiles(mainRoot, base, branch) {
  const r = git.git(['diff', '--name-only', `${base}...${branch}`], mainRoot);
  return r.ok ? r.stdout.split('\n').map((s) => s.trim()).filter(Boolean) : [];
}

/**
 * Default squash-commit message when --message is absent.
 *
 * The subject comes from the branch's HIGHEST-WEIGHT commit, not its newest one — see lib/squash.js
 * for why (short version: the newest commit on a well-run branch is the docs pass, so features
 * shipped titled `docs:` and vanished from the changelog). The body keeps the design that was never
 * at fault: `Closes #N` per claimed issue, the other subjects as bullets, `chore(sync)` filtered,
 * Co-Authored-By / Claude-Session trailers preserved.
 *
 * Returns '' when `branchCommits` found nothing the branch wrote itself (#57) — composeSquashMessage
 * is defined to return '' for an empty commit list. Callers must treat that as "no subject to
 * derive", never as "empty string, use it" — see the two call sites below.
 */
function buildSquashMessage(mainRoot, trunk, branch, closes, refs = [], conflicts) {
  return squash.composeSquashMessage(branchCommits(mainRoot, trunk, branch), closes, refs, conflicts);
}

const HELP_SHIP = `colab ship [--repo <path>] [--worktree <name> | --branch <br>] [--message "<msg>"] [--refs <N[,M]>] [--keep-worktree] [--delete-branch] [--dry] [--json]

The ONE sanctioned door for code-wrap Phase B: squash-merge a session branch into the BASE IT WAS
CUT FROM — trunk for an ordinary session, or the declared integration line recorded on its worktree
(project.yml \`integration:\`). Autonomy is granted by the repo's .github/project.yml
(\`autonomy: auto-trunk\`) — NOT by any flag. Without that grant, ship refuses and a human must run
Phase B. ship NEVER touches main (when trunk≠main), NEVER tags, NEVER promotes.

Base and target are ONE decision. A branch cut from a long-lived line and shipped into trunk would
drag the entire line in behind it, as a single squash commit that reads like a small change — so
ship follows the recorded base and refuses to retarget. Shipping a declared LINE into trunk is
refused outright, even under autonomy: auto-trunk: a long divergence meeting the branch that
promotes is an integration event of the same weight as a promotion, and stays human.

Sequence (each step gated; any failure aborts before the push — the target is never half-shipped):
  a. autonomy gate      repo must grant auto-trunk (no --force override)
  b. preconditions      target CI alive+green · no new migrations on branch (UNLESS every issue the
                        branch carries holds a valid \`colab migration-grant\`, #98 — see below) ·
                        trunk checkout clean · every closing claim corroborated by git (see below)
                        (an integration line with NO runs at all borrows trunk's verdict, advisory —
                        an ungated line is a normal early state; a RED line still blocks)
  c. B0 sync            merge the target INTO the branch; non-generated conflict → abort (human merge);
                        generated-only conflict → repo's .colab/hooks/pre-ship regenerates, else abort
  d. B1 squash          re-check CI green, then squash-merge branch → target (Closes every claimed issue,
                        except a REFERENCED one — see --refs / the "tracking" label below — which gets
                        Refs #N and stays open). Into trunk that happens in the main checkout; into a
                        line it happens in an EPHEMERAL worktree, so the main checkout never leaves trunk.
  e. B2 push            push the target (COLAB_SHIP=1 in env so a pre-push-guard lets it through)
  f. B3 teardown        colab worktree rm (releases claims + ports + ✅ comments) unless --keep-worktree.
                        The BRANCH IS KEPT — pass --delete-branch to remove it (local + remote).
  g. B4 verify + h.     confirm each issue auto-closed; post "🚢 Shipped to <trunk>" per issue
  i. group teardown     for every group:<key> label the branch's issues carried, delete the
                        LABEL OBJECT once no open issue carries it anymore (#82) — a live member
                        elsewhere leaves it in place

CLAIMS ARE CROSS-CHECKED AGAINST GIT before anything closes (#87). The set ship would close comes
from the claim registry, read AT SHIP TIME — so a claim a co-tenant session wrote onto this branch
minutes ago is indistinguishable from one this session made. Measured: a branch carrying #71 and
#76 reported issues [71, 74, 76], because a second session claimed #74 onto the same worktree after
the ship was authorised and had only just begun; nothing on the branch implemented it. ship now
asks git whether the BRANCH NAME's trailing group or any COMMIT BODY names each closing issue, and
refuses when neither does. Fix it by deciding what is true — \`colab release <N>\` if the claim is
not this branch's work, \`--refs <N>\` if it is related but not completed here — never by weakening
the check: a wrong \`Closes #N\` closes the issue the instant trunk is pushed and stays in an
immutable commit message afterwards.

MIGRATION GRANTS (#98) are the ONE narrow, human-created exception to the no-new-migrations
precondition — for an issue whose entire deliverable IS a schema change, which the gate would
otherwise make permanently un-shippable unattended. Create one with
\`colab migration-grant <N> --branch <br>\` (requires COLAB_HUMAN=1 — the same bar \`colab promote\`
holds a production promotion to; nothing else, no flag, no project.yml field, can grant one). A
grant is bound to ONE issue and ONE branch, and expires the instant that issue closes. On a group
branch EVERY claimed issue needs a valid grant for THIS branch — one granted issue never smuggles
an unreviewed migration in for its siblings. Revoke with \`--revoke\` (same COLAB_HUMAN=1 bar);
list what is outstanding with \`--list\`. See \`colab migration-grant --help\` for the full design.

The default squash subject is the branch's HIGHEST-WEIGHT commit (breaking > feat > fix > perf >
refactor > docs > test > chore, ties to the oldest) — NOT its newest commit, which on a well-run
branch is the docs pass and used to retitle whole features as \`docs:\`, hiding them from the
release notes. The body is unchanged: Closes per issue, other subjects as bullets, footers kept.

Two ADVISORY subject checks run before the irreversible step (#88), and neither blocks: a
type-mismatch (the chosen subject's type disagrees with the branch's own \`feat/\`/\`fix/\` prefix)
and a narrow-subject (the titling commit touched a small fraction of the files the branch changed).
Both catch the same measured failure from opposite sides — a branch shipping a convention plus +104
lines of docs landed titled "fix(labels): correct mechanical-readiness tests…", because its only
commit describing the deliverable was typed \`wip:\` and was therefore invisible to the ranking.
Pass --message to title it by hand.

EVIDENCE-CLOSE — a branch that finished with NO diff (#90). A design-consult session, an
investigation concluding "no change needed", or a deliverable stored outside the repo ends with a
real result and zero commits. That branch cannot be squashed (there is nothing to stage) and used to
have no completion path at all: the claim was released, the worktree torn down, and the issue stayed
open forever, because the only close mechanism in the system was \`Closes #N\` inside a squash commit.
ship now detects \`landed AND zero own commits\` — both measured from git, never declared by the
session — and switches to evidence-close: post an evidence comment, CLOSE each issue, tear down. No
merge, no push, and deliberately NO --allow-empty marker commit, which would put a lie in the
history of every repo that adopts this. The gate: an issue is closed this way only when it ALREADY
carries a comment colab did not write (its own 🔒/✅/🚢/🔖 markers do not count). An issue without one
is reported and LEFT OPEN while the rest proceed.

B3 KEEPS the branch by default. Deleting refs from a shared remote is not something this command
does unasked, however certain it is that the content landed: reporting is safe and reversible,
deleting is neither. The accepted cost is that shipped branches accumulate — and because a squash
leaves no ancestry, \`git branch --merged\` can never list them, so \`colab doctor\` is where you
find them. Treat that list as routine maintenance, not as an alarm.

\`--delete-branch\` opts in: local + remote, only AFTER trunk is pushed, with \`branch -D\` (a
squashed branch is not "merged" as far as git is concerned). Best-effort — a failure warns rather
than failing a ship that already succeeded. Passing it together with --keep-worktree is refused
with a warning, because git will not delete a branch that is still checked out.

Options:
  --repo <path>       repo root (default: git root of cwd)
  --worktree <name>   the session worktree to ship (its branch + claims are used)
  --branch <br>       ship by branch name (its worktree, if any, is found in state)
  --message "<msg>"   subject for the squash commit (Closes/Refs trailer is always spliced in)
  --refs <N[,M]>      reference these claimed issues (Refs #N) instead of closing them — for a
                      long-lived memory/tracking issue the branch touched but did not complete.
                      Comma-separated; each must be a claimed issue on the branch. An issue carrying
                      the "tracking" label is treated this way automatically, no flag needed. The
                      claim is still released either way — only Closes→Refs changes.
  --keep-worktree     do not remove the worktree after shipping
  --delete-branch     delete the branch (local + remote) after shipping — off by default
  --dry               print the plan + precondition table and change NOTHING
  --json              with --dry ONLY: emit ONE machine-readable JSON object instead of the prose
                      table, for an unattended caller (#70's scheduled drivers) that must act on the
                      answer mechanically. Exit-code semantics and the plain --dry prose output are
                      UNCHANGED by this flag — it is additive, never a second way to read the same run.

                        { "ok": bool, "dry": true, "repo": "...", "branch": "...", "target": "...",
                          "worktree": "..." | null, "mode": "squash" | "evidence-close",
                          "issues": [N, ...],
                          "closeIssues": [N, ...], "refsIssues": [N, ...],
                          "checklistFindings": [ { "issue": N, "unchecked": n, "total": n, "reason": "..." } ],
                          "checks": [ { "name": "...", "ok": bool, "class": "..." | null, "detail": "..." } ] }

                      \`mode\` says which completion path this branch has (#90). "evidence-close"
                      means there is nothing to merge and ship will close the issues from evidence
                      instead — an unattended caller must NOT read it as a no-op.

                      \`checks\` has one entry per precondition — branch resolves, not an integration
                      line, autonomy granted, target CI alive+green, no new migrations, trunk
                      checkout ready, no hand-merge conflict (a real preview merge, done in a
                      throwaway detached worktree — nothing on disk here is touched by it), claims
                      corroborated by git (#87), and branch has commits (#90 — TRUE, not false, when
                      the branch qualifies for evidence-close: it is a different path, not a
                      failure). \`class\`
                      is set ONLY on a failing check, from a CLOSED, STABLE-WIRE-FORMAT vocabulary:
                        self-clearing   CI still running, a transient gh/preview read failure — an
                                        unattended caller may retry later with NO human action.
                        human-gated     autonomy absent, an unresolvable branch record, a declared
                                        integration line, a real CI failure, new migrations without a
                                        valid grant on every claimed issue (#98), a dirty trunk
                                        checkout, a genuine merge conflict — retrying changes nothing
                                        until a person acts.
                      A branch whose migrations are ALL granted reports \`no new migrations\` as an
                      ORDINARY PASS (ok:true, class:null) — a grant does not add a third vocabulary
                      value, it makes the check pass, the same as having no migrations at all.
                      Never add a third value without updating every consumer of this field.

                      \`closeIssues\`/\`refsIssues\` (#74) are \`issues\` split by what a real ship would
                      write: Closes vs Refs, unioning --refs, the "tracking" label, and the close gate.
                      \`checklistFindings\` lists only the issues the CLOSE GATE (not --refs, not the
                      label) moved from close to ref — an unticked \`## Plan\` checklist with no
                      declared \`Remainder: #M\`. Deliberately NOT folded into \`checks\`/\`ok\`: a
                      checklist finding does not block this ship, it only redirects that one issue's
                      trailer, so it must never read as a precondition failure to an unattended caller.

                      \`migrationGrant\` (#98) is \`null\` when the branch adds no migration file (the
                      ordinary case). Otherwise: \`{ branch, files: [...], granted: [{issue,by,at}],
                      missing: [{issue,reason}] }\` — validated over EVERY issue the branch carries
                      (\`issues\`, not \`closeIssues\`: \`--refs\` must never shrink a safety gate), so a
                      group branch with one ungranted member reports \`ok:false\` here with that
                      member named in \`missing\`, even though the OTHERS are fully granted.
`;

/**
 * B3 (second half): what happens to the branch whose content has just landed on trunk.
 *
 * THE DEFAULT IS TO KEEP IT. Deleting requires an explicit `--delete-branch`.
 *
 * This premise has now been wrong twice in opposite directions, so state it exactly. Originally a
 * comment in buildSquashMessage justified preserving the newest commit verbatim by claiming "the
 * branch is deleted at B3" — nothing in the CLI deleted anything, so the branch always survived.
 * That was then fixed by making B3 delete. The operator reversed it: an agent removing refs from a
 * SHARED REMOTE is the wrong default however well-verified the deletion is, because reporting is
 * safe and reversible while deleting is neither. So the branch survives again — but now by
 * decision, documented, with an opt-in, instead of by an unimplemented comment.
 *
 * The consequence is accepted deliberately: shipped branches accumulate. `git branch --merged
 * <trunk>` cannot find them (a squash leaves no ancestry, which is also why deletion needs `-D`
 * rather than `-d`), so `colab doctor` reporting them is the mitigation, not a backstop.
 *
 * When deletion IS requested: it runs only after B2 pushed trunk, so the content is durable on the
 * remote before the ref goes. It refuses to touch trunk or `main` under any circumstances, and
 * every failure is a warning — a protected branch or a missing remote must not fail a ship that
 * already succeeded.
 */
function handleShippedBranch(repoAbs, trunk, branch, { deleteBranch = false, worktreeKept = false, integration = [] } = {}) {
  if (!branch) return;
  if (!deleteBranch) {
    print(`  keeping branch ${branch} (default; --delete-branch to remove it, or \`colab doctor\` to list what has accumulated)`);
    return;
  }
  if (branch === trunk || branch === 'main' || integration.includes(branch)) { // belt-and-braces; cmdShip already refuses these
    warn(`refusing to delete "${branch}" — it is the trunk, main, or a declared integration line`);
    return;
  }
  if (worktreeKept) {
    // Both flags passed. git will not delete a checked-out branch, so honour the safer one and say
    // why, rather than attempting a delete that is guaranteed to fail.
    warn(`--delete-branch ignored for ${branch}: its worktree was kept (--keep-worktree), so the branch is still checked out`);
    return;
  }
  const del = git.git(['branch', '-D', branch], repoAbs);
  if (del.ok) print(`  deleted local branch ${branch}`);
  else warn(`could not delete local branch ${branch}: ${del.stderr.split('\n')[0] || 'unknown error'}`);

  // Remote: only if it is actually there. Checked against the local remote-tracking ref rather than
  // the network, so an offline ship still finishes instead of hanging on ls-remote.
  const onRemote = git.git(['rev-parse', '--verify', '--quiet', `refs/remotes/origin/${branch}`], repoAbs).ok;
  if (!onRemote) return;
  const rdel = git.run('git', ['push', 'origin', '--delete', branch], { cwd: repoAbs, env: { ...process.env, COLAB_SHIP: '1' } });
  if (rdel.ok) print(`  deleted origin/${branch}`);
  else warn(`could not delete origin/${branch}: ${rdel.stderr.split('\n')[0] || 'unknown error'} — delete it by hand`);
}

/** Resolve the session (branch, worktree name/path, claimed issue numbers) from --worktree/--branch. */
function resolveShipSession(st, repoAbs, opts) {
  let name = null, branch = null, wtPath = null;
  let session = '', sessionName = '';
  if (opts.worktree) {
    const wt = st.worktrees[opts.worktree];
    if (!wt) throw new UserError(`No worktree "${opts.worktree}" in state`);
    name = wt.name; branch = wt.branch; wtPath = wt.path; session = wt.session || ''; sessionName = wt.sessionName || '';
  } else if (opts.branch) {
    branch = opts.branch;
    const wt = Object.values(st.worktrees).find((w) => w.repo === repoAbs && w.branch === branch);
    if (wt) { name = wt.name; wtPath = wt.path; session = wt.session || ''; sessionName = wt.sessionName || ''; }
  } else {
    throw new UserError('ship needs --worktree <name> or --branch <br>');
  }
  // Claimed issues for this session: worktree-keyed AND branch-keyed claims are UNIONED (#76).
  // `colab claim <N> --worktree <w> --branch <b>` writes worktree === w; the warning's own printed
  // remedy — `colab claim <N> --branch <b>` (no --worktree) — writes worktree === null instead, and
  // used to be invisible here once a worktree name was known, because the old filter picked ONE key
  // space and stayed there: `name ? c.worktree === name : c.branch === branch`. A branch-keyed claim
  // and a worktree-keyed claim on the SAME branch name the same work, so both count.
  const claims = Object.values(st.claims)
    .filter((c) => c.repo === repoAbs && ((name && c.worktree === name) || (branch && c.branch === branch)));
  const issues = [...new Set(claims.map((c) => normIssueNum(c.issue)))].sort((a, b) => a - b);
  if (!session && !sessionName) { // fall back to a claim's identity
    const withId = claims.find((c) => c.session || c.sessionName);
    if (withId) { session = withId.session || ''; sessionName = withId.sessionName || ''; }
  }
  return { name, branch, wtPath, issues, session, sessionName };
}

// The closed refusal-class vocabulary for `ship --dry --json` (#77) — a STABLE WIRE FORMAT, same
// posture as the CLAIM_MARK/RELEASE_MARK comment markers above. An unattended caller (a scheduled
// driver, #70) needs to tell "retry later, unattended" apart from "park it, a human must act" — the
// two options it has today, parsing stderr prose or re-deriving the checks itself, both drift.
const SELF_CLEARING = 'self-clearing'; // CI still running, a transient gh/preview read failure
const HUMAN_GATED = 'human-gated';     // autonomy absent, a real failure, a decision only a person makes

/**
 * Splits a branch's claimed issues into CLOSE (`Closes #N`) and REF (`Refs #N`, kept open) sets,
 * unioning THREE independent reasons an issue must stay open — #48's original two, plus #74's
 * close gate:
 *   - `--refs N[,M]`         explicit, per-ship
 *   - the `tracking` label    declarative, durable across every session touching the domain
 *   - an unticked `## Plan` checklist with no declared `Remainder: #M` (tools/lib/checklist.js) —
 *     the issue's OWN recorded scope says it is not done. This is the one #74 exists for: the
 *     incident was an issue closed by squash-merge with a third of its scope unimplemented, and
 *     the scope was prose so nothing could have caught it mechanically. A checklist can be.
 *
 * `gh` unusable (no auth / no origin) degrades this to `--refs` only, same "degrade, never gate"
 * posture the tracking-label check already had — nothing can be read, so nothing new is asserted,
 * rather than refusing to ship over a read this function never attempted.
 *
 * Returns `gateFindings`: one entry per issue the CHECKLIST gate — not the flag, not the label —
 * moved from close to ref. Callers report these distinctly: unlike an explicit `--refs` or a
 * declared tracking issue, this one is a surprise the operator did not ask for, and the whole
 * point of #74 is that it must be LOUD.
 */
function resolveCloseRefsSplit(repoAbs, gh, issues, refsFromFlag) {
  const refsSet = new Set(issues.filter((n) => refsFromFlag.has(n)));
  const gateFindings = [];
  if (gh) {
    for (const num of issues) {
      if (refsSet.has(num)) continue;
      const info = git.ghIssueView(repoAbs, num, ['labels']);
      const names = info && Array.isArray(info.labels) ? info.labels.map((l) => l.name) : [];
      if (names.includes(labels.TRACKING_LABEL)) refsSet.add(num);
    }
    // #74: the close gate. Only asked of issues that would otherwise CLOSE — one already kept
    // open by --refs or the tracking label was never going to close anyway, so gating it a second
    // way is pure noise.
    for (const num of issues) {
      if (refsSet.has(num)) continue;
      const info = git.ghIssueView(repoAbs, num, ['body', 'comments']);
      if (!info) continue; // read failed — degrade, never gate on a read we could not attempt
      const gate = checklist.closeGate(info.body, info.comments);
      if (!gate.ok) {
        refsSet.add(num);
        gateFindings.push({ issue: num, unchecked: gate.unchecked, total: gate.total, reason: gate.reason });
      }
    }
  }
  const closeIssues = issues.filter((n) => !refsSet.has(n));
  const refsIssues = issues.filter((n) => refsSet.has(n));
  return { closeIssues, refsIssues, refsSet, gateFindings };
}

/**
 * CI precondition for `ship --dry --json`, classified into the closed vocabulary above. Mirrors the
 * prose path's rule exactly (same fallback: an integration line with NO runs at all borrows trunk's
 * verdict, advisory) so the two never disagree about whether CI is green — only about how the
 * reason for "no" is labelled.
 */
function shipCiCheck(repoAbs, gh, target, trunk) {
  const name = `${target === trunk ? 'trunk' : 'line'} CI green`;
  if (!gh) return { name, ok: false, class: SELF_CLEARING, detail: 'gh not usable (no auth / no origin) — cannot confirm CI' };
  let run = git.ghRunForSha(repoAbs, target);
  let via = target;
  if (run && run.status === 'none' && target !== trunk) {
    run = git.ghRunForSha(repoAbs, trunk);
    via = `${trunk} (line "${target}" has no runs — ungated, advisory)`;
  }
  if (!run) return { name, ok: false, class: SELF_CLEARING, detail: 'gh run list failed' };
  const at = `${via}@${shortSha(run.sha)}`;
  if (run.status === 'completed') {
    if (run.conclusion === 'success') return { name, ok: true, class: null, detail: `run for ${at}: success` };
    return { name, ok: false, class: HUMAN_GATED, detail: `run for ${at}: status=completed conclusion=${run.conclusion || 'none'}` };
  }
  if (run.status === 'none') return { name, ok: false, class: HUMAN_GATED, detail: `no run for ${at} — CI is not wired for this ref, or never started for this commit` };
  return { name, ok: false, class: SELF_CLEARING, detail: `run for ${at}: status=${run.status} — still running, retry later` };
}

/**
 * Previews whether B0's merge (target INTO the branch) would conflict — WITHOUT touching the real
 * worktree, the branch, or the network beyond a fetch — so `ship --dry --json`'s "no hand-merge
 * conflict" check is a real measurement, not a guess. Today's plain `--dry` never attempts this at
 * all: a hand-merge conflict is discovered only by running a real `colab ship` and hitting B0 (#77).
 *
 * Mirrors B0's own generated-file exemption exactly (isGeneratedPath + .colab/hooks/pre-ship), so
 * this verdict and the outcome a real ship would reach never disagree.
 *
 * Runs in a throwaway DETACHED worktree so nothing here can move the branch or the main checkout —
 * the same invariant every other worktree use in this file honours. `unknown: true` means the
 * preview itself could not be attempted (disk, a git too old, a locked repo); callers must NOT read
 * unknown as a clean merge.
 */
function shipMergeConflictCheck(repoAbs, target, branch, generated, hasPreShip) {
  const tmp = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'colab-ship-preview-'));
  const add = git.git(['worktree', 'add', '--detach', tmp, branch], repoAbs);
  if (!add.ok) {
    try { fs.rmdirSync(tmp); } catch (_) { /* best-effort */ }
    return { ok: false, unknown: true, detail: `could not preview the merge: ${add.stderr.split('\n')[0] || 'worktree add failed'}` };
  }
  try {
    git.git(['fetch', 'origin', target], tmp);
    let mergeRef = `origin/${target}`;
    if (!git.git(['rev-parse', '--verify', '--quiet', mergeRef], tmp).ok) mergeRef = target;
    const merge = git.git(['merge', '--no-commit', '--no-edit', mergeRef], tmp);
    const uf = git.git(['diff', '--name-only', '--diff-filter=U'], tmp).stdout.split('\n').filter(Boolean);
    git.git(['merge', '--abort'], tmp); // harmless no-op when the merge already finished clean
    if (uf.length === 0) {
      if (merge.ok) return { ok: true, unknown: false, detail: `clean merge into ${target}` };
      return { ok: false, unknown: true, detail: `merge could not be previewed: ${(merge.stderr || 'unknown reason').split('\n')[0]}` };
    }
    const nonGen = uf.filter((f) => !isGeneratedPath(f, generated));
    if (nonGen.length) return { ok: false, unknown: false, detail: `${nonGen.length} conflict(s) in non-generated file(s): ${nonGen.join(', ')}` };
    if (!hasPreShip) return { ok: false, unknown: false, detail: `generated-file conflict(s) with no .colab/hooks/pre-ship to resolve them: ${uf.join(', ')}` };
    return { ok: true, unknown: false, detail: `only generated-file conflict(s), auto-resolved at B0 by .colab/hooks/pre-ship: ${uf.join(', ')}` };
  } finally {
    git.git(['worktree', 'remove', '--force', tmp], repoAbs);
  }
}

/**
 * `ship --dry --json` — the whole feature, isolated in its own function on purpose (#77). The prose
 * `--dry` path just below stays byte-identical to before this landed; this does its own resolution
 * rather than threading a json branch through it, so the two can never accidentally cross-affect
 * each other's output.
 *
 * Unlike the prose path's three hard early-refusals (branch doesn't resolve / is an integration
 * line / autonomy absent), autonomy failing here does NOT stop the remaining checks from being
 * computed — an unattended caller wants the WHOLE picture in one read, not one reason at a time.
 * Branch-doesn't-resolve and is-an-integration-line still short-circuit: everything downstream
 * (target, CI, migrations) is meaningless without a real branch to ship.
 */
function cmdShipDryJson(opts) {
  const repoAbs = resolveRepo(opts);
  if (!git.repoRoot(repoAbs)) throw new UserError(`${repoAbs} is not a git repo`);
  const doc = projectDoc(repoAbs);
  const st = state.loadState();
  const sess = resolveShipSession(st, repoAbs, opts);
  const trunk = resolveTrunk(repoAbs, doc);
  if (!trunk) throw new UserError('Could not determine trunk branch (set project.yml trunk:)');
  if (sess.branch === trunk) throw new UserError(`Refusing: --branch is the trunk itself (${trunk}) — nothing to ship`);

  const checks = [];
  let target = null;
  // #74: the close-gate split (closeIssues/refsIssues/checklistFindings) is reported ALONGSIDE
  // `checks`, never folded into it — a checklist-gated issue does not block this ship (it only
  // redirects that one issue's Closes to a Refs), so it must not flip `ok` the way a real
  // precondition failure does. `checks`' documented contract (HELP_SHIP) is "one entry per
  // PRECONDITION, ok:false only on something that blocks" — additive new top-level fields keep
  // that contract intact for every existing consumer of `ok`/`checks`.
  let closeIssues = sess.issues, refsIssues = [], checklistFindings = [];
  // #90: `mode` is additive and tells an unattended caller WHICH completion path this branch has —
  // "squash" or "evidence-close". Without it, a zero-commit branch read `ok: true` and then hit
  // B1's wall, because `checks[]` had no "branch has commits" precondition at all.
  let mode = 'squash';
  // #98: null when the branch carries no migration files (the ordinary case) — set alongside the
  // 'no new migrations' check, below, the moment migrations are found.
  let migrationGrantPayload = null;
  const emit = (ok) => {
    print(JSON.stringify({
      ok, dry: true, repo: shortRepo(repoAbs), branch: sess.branch, worktree: sess.name || null,
      target, mode, issues: sess.issues, closeIssues, refsIssues, checklistFindings, checks,
      migrationGrant: migrationGrantPayload,
    }, null, 2));
    return ok ? 0 : 1;
  };

  // ---- the recorded branch must RESOLVE, before anything else is computed (mirrors the prose gate) ----
  if (!git.branchExists(repoAbs, sess.branch)) {
    const shown = sess.branch === null || sess.branch === undefined ? 'null (no branch)' : `"${sess.branch}"`;
    checks.push({ name: 'branch resolves', ok: false, class: HUMAN_GATED,
      detail: `the branch recorded for this session is ${shown} — it resolves to no ref` });
    return emit(false);
  }
  checks.push({ name: 'branch resolves', ok: true, class: null, detail: `"${sess.branch}"` });

  // ---- the branch itself must not be a declared integration line ----
  const integration = resolveIntegration(repoAbs, doc, trunk);
  if (integration.includes(sess.branch)) {
    checks.push({ name: 'not an integration line', ok: false, class: HUMAN_GATED,
      detail: `"${sess.branch}" is a declared integration line (project.yml integration:) — merging it into ${trunk} is a human act` });
    return emit(false);
  }
  checks.push({ name: 'not an integration line', ok: true, class: null, detail: 'ordinary session branch' });

  // ---- the recorded base must still be declared ----
  target = resolveBase(st, repoAbs, sess.name, trunk);
  if (target !== trunk && !integration.includes(target)) {
    checks.push({ name: 'declared base', ok: false, class: HUMAN_GATED,
      detail: `recorded base "${target}" is no longer a declared base (allowed: ${allowedBases(repoAbs, doc, trunk).join(', ')})` });
    return emit(false);
  }
  checks.push({ name: 'declared base', ok: true, class: null, detail: target });

  // ---- (a) AUTONOMY GATE — does NOT short-circuit here; an unattended caller wants the whole table ----
  const autonomy = doc.autonomy ? String(doc.autonomy) : 'manual';
  const autonomyOk = autonomy === 'auto-trunk';
  checks.push({ name: 'autonomy granted', ok: autonomyOk, class: autonomyOk ? null : HUMAN_GATED,
    detail: autonomyOk ? 'auto-trunk' : `project.yml autonomy: ${doc.autonomy ? autonomy : 'absent'} — a human must trigger Phase B` });

  // ---- (b) the rest of the preconditions, same rules as the prose path ----
  const gh = isGhUsable(repoAbs);
  checks.push(shipCiCheck(repoAbs, gh, target, trunk));

  // #74: same close-gate split the prose path runs, reported via the additive fields above.
  const refsFromFlagJson = new Set(
    String(opts.refs || '').split(',').map((s) => s.trim().replace(/^#/, '')).filter(Boolean).map(Number),
  );
  const split = resolveCloseRefsSplit(repoAbs, gh, sess.issues, refsFromFlagJson);
  closeIssues = split.closeIssues; refsIssues = split.refsIssues; checklistFindings = split.gateFindings;

  const migrations = newMigrations(repoAbs, target, sess.branch);
  const migGrants = migrations.length ? shipMigrationGrants(repoAbs, gh, sess.issues, sess.branch) : null;
  const migOk = migrations.length === 0 || migGrants.ok;
  const migDetailed = migDetail(migrations, migGrants, target, sess.branch);
  migrationGrantPayload = migDetailed.payload;
  checks.push({ name: 'no new migrations', ok: migOk, class: migOk ? null : HUMAN_GATED,
    detail: migDetailed.text });

  const headBranch = git.git(['rev-parse', '--abbrev-ref', 'HEAD'], repoAbs).stdout;
  const onTrunk = headBranch === trunk;
  // dirtyTracked, NOT dirtyAny — deliberate, and #86 did not change it. The two questions differ:
  //   `worktree rm` asks "am I about to DESTROY something with no other copy?" → untracked counts,
  //     because that is precisely the category with nowhere to be restored from.
  //   this asks "is the trunk checkout fit to merge INTO?" → untracked does not count. A long-lived
  //     trunk checkout accumulates scratch files and un-ignored local config indefinitely, and none
  //     of it is at risk: a merge does not delete untracked files, and where an incoming path would
  //     collide git refuses the merge itself rather than clobbering. Gating on it here would block
  //     ship on every repo carrying a stray artifact, to protect against nothing.
  // So this is not an oversight to "finish" — completing the sweep here is the regression.
  const dirty = git.dirtyTracked(repoAbs);
  const treeOk = onTrunk && !dirty;
  checks.push({ name: 'trunk checkout ready', ok: treeOk, class: treeOk ? null : HUMAN_GATED,
    detail: !onTrunk ? `main checkout is on "${headBranch}", not "${trunk}"` : (dirty ? 'tracked changes uncommitted in trunk checkout' : `on ${trunk}, clean`) });

  // ---- (c) no hand-merge conflict — NEW: the plain --dry prose path never checks this today ----
  const generated = [...DEFAULT_GENERATED, ...(Array.isArray(doc.generated) ? doc.generated.map(String) : [])];
  const hasPreShip = fs.existsSync(path.join(repoAbs, '.colab', 'hooks', 'pre-ship'));
  const mc = shipMergeConflictCheck(repoAbs, target, sess.branch, generated, hasPreShip);
  checks.push({ name: 'no hand-merge conflict', ok: mc.ok, class: mc.ok ? null : (mc.unknown ? SELF_CLEARING : HUMAN_GATED), detail: mc.detail });

  // ---- (d) the two checks the machine-facing path was missing entirely ----
  const dryCommits = branchCommits(repoAbs, target, sess.branch);

  // #87: B1b's cross-check, same rule as the prose path — only over the issues that would CLOSE.
  const dryCorr = shipguard.corroborateIssues(closeIssues, sess.branch, dryCommits);
  checks.push({ name: 'claims corroborated by git', ok: dryCorr.ok, class: dryCorr.ok ? null : HUMAN_GATED,
    detail: dryCorr.ok
      ? (closeIssues.length ? `${closeIssues.length} closing issue(s) named by the branch or its commits` : 'none to close')
      : `${dryCorr.uncorroborated.map((u) => '#' + u.issue).join(' ')} claimed but named by NEITHER the branch name nor any commit body — a co-tenant claim would be closed by this ship` });

  // #90: "branch has commits" — the precondition whose absence told an unattended caller `ok: true`
  // about a branch that could not ship. It is NOT a failure when the branch qualifies for
  // evidence-close; it is a different mode, and saying so is the whole point.
  const dryMode = shipMode(repoAbs, target, sess.branch, dryCommits);
  if (dryMode.evidenceClose) {
    mode = 'evidence-close';
    checks.push({ name: 'branch has commits', ok: true, class: null,
      detail: `zero commits, and ${dryMode.why} — completion path is evidence-close (no merge, no push)` });
  } else if (dryCommits.length === 0) {
    checks.push({ name: 'branch has commits', ok: false, class: HUMAN_GATED,
      detail: `no commits of its own vs ${target}, and it does not qualify for evidence-close ` +
        `(landed verdict: ${dryMode.verdict.state} — ${dryMode.verdict.why}); a squash would stage nothing` });
  } else {
    checks.push({ name: 'branch has commits', ok: true, class: null, detail: `${dryCommits.length} vs ${target}` });
  }

  return emit(checks.every((c) => c.ok));
}

/**
 * Which completion path a branch has (#90): the ordinary squash-merge, or `evidence-close`.
 *
 * Evidence-close is for a branch that finished with a real deliverable and NO diff — a decision
 * recorded on its issue, an investigation concluding "no change needed", an artifact stored outside
 * the repo. It is not exotic; design-consult and investigation sessions end this way routinely.
 *
 * BOTH conditions are required, and they are not the same question:
 *   - `landed` (lib/landed.js) — merging this branch would not change the base's tree;
 *   - zero commits of its own — nothing on the branch's first-parent chain past the merge-base.
 *
 * A branch that is `landed` WITH commits is the ordinary squash-already-merged case, which has its
 * own handling and must not be diverted here. Requiring both keeps this path to the shape it was
 * ruled for, and both come from git — the session never declares its own zero-diff-ness.
 *
 * `unknown` is never evidence-close. lib/landed.js is explicit that `unknown` means "look", and
 * routing an unmeasurable branch into a path that CLOSES ISSUES WITHOUT MERGING is exactly the
 * direction that must fail safe.
 */
function shipMode(repoAbs, target, branch, commits) {
  const verdict = landed.landedState(repoAbs, target, branch);
  const zeroCommits = Array.isArray(commits) && commits.length === 0;
  if (verdict.state === 'landed' && zeroCommits) {
    return { evidenceClose: true, verdict, why: `landed vs ${target} (${verdict.why}) and zero commits of its own` };
  }
  return { evidenceClose: false, verdict, why: null };
}

/**
 * The evidence-close path (#90). Posts evidence, CLOSES each closing issue, then tears down exactly
 * as B3 does. No merge, no commit, no push — and deliberately no `--allow-empty` marker commit:
 * inventing a commit to satisfy a code path would put a lie in the history of every repo that
 * adopts this.
 *
 * THE GATE, and why it is this one. This is the first `gh issue close` in the CLI. Until now the
 * tool expressed "this issue is done" exclusively as a commit trailer, which has a genuinely nice
 * property: the tracker never moves unless trunk moved. Closing without a merge breaks that
 * symmetry on purpose, so something has to replace it — an issue may be closed here only when it
 * ALREADY carries a comment colab did not write (lib/shipguard.js `hasEvidence`). That mirrors the
 * rule an adopting repo already uses for non-code delivery: closed ∧ evidence comment ⇒ landed.
 *
 * An issue with no evidence is REPORTED AND LEFT OPEN while the rest proceed. The alternative —
 * refusing the whole ship — would put the session back in the stall this exists to end, and the
 * other issues' evidence is not made worse by this one's absence.
 */
function shipEvidenceClose({ repoAbs, st, sess, target, trunk, gh, closeIssues, refsIssues, refsSet, mode, opts }) {
  print(`E1: evidence-close — no merge into ${target}, nothing to push.`);
  if (!gh) {
    process.stderr.write('✗ E1: gh is not usable (no auth / no origin), so neither the evidence check nor the\n');
    process.stderr.write('  close can be performed. Nothing was changed. Fix gh and re-run.\n');
    return 1;
  }

  const head = git.git(['rev-parse', '--short', sess.branch], repoAbs).stdout || '(unknown)';
  const closed = [];
  const skipped = [];
  for (const num of closeIssues) {
    const info = git.ghIssueView(repoAbs, num, ['comments', 'state']);
    if (!info) { skipped.push({ num, why: 'could not read the issue (gh)' }); continue; }
    if (info.state === 'CLOSED') { closed.push(num); continue; } // already done — not a failure
    if (!shipguard.hasEvidence(info.comments)) {
      skipped.push({ num, why: 'no evidence comment — only colab\'s own markers' });
      continue;
    }
    const shipSess = sessionCommentSuffix(sess.session, sess.sessionName);
    const body = `✅ Closed by colab ship — **evidence-close**: this branch (\`${sess.branch}\` @ ${head}) ` +
      `introduces no change to \`${target}\`, so there is no merge commit and no \`Closes #${num}\` to carry.\n\n` +
      `The deliverable is recorded on this issue (see the comment(s) above), which is what authorised ` +
      `the close — colab closes a zero-diff issue only when it already carries a comment colab did not ` +
      `write. The zero-diff fact was measured from git: ${mode.why}.${shipSess}`;
    const c = git.ghIssueComment(repoAbs, num, body);
    if (!c.ok) warn(`could not post the evidence-close comment on #${num} — closing anyway`);
    const r = git.run('gh', ['issue', 'close', String(num), '--reason', 'completed'], { cwd: repoAbs });
    if (!r.ok) { skipped.push({ num, why: `gh issue close failed: ${(r.stderr || '').split('\n')[0]}` }); continue; }
    closed.push(num);
    print(`  #${num} closed with evidence.`);
  }

  for (const s of skipped) {
    warn(`#${s.num} left OPEN — ${s.why}.`);
    warn('  Record what was delivered as a comment on the issue, then re-run; colab will not invent');
    warn('  the evidence for you, and closing without it is what the gate exists to prevent.');
  }
  for (const num of refsIssues) print(`  #${num} kept open (ref).`);

  // The same events the merge path emits, minus the merge: there is no sha, so `issue.merged` — whose
  // payload IS the merge commit — is deliberately not sent. A receiver must not be told a merge
  // happened. The ship event carries the state honestly instead.
  pushEvent('ship', {
    repo: repoAbs, worktree: sess.name, session: sess.session,
    issue: sess.issues.length === 1 ? sess.issues[0] : undefined,
    payload: { state: 'evidence-closed', branch: sess.branch, target, sha: null, issues: closed },
  });

  // Teardown, exactly as B3 — the claims are released by worktree rm, as on every other path.
  if (sess.name && !opts.keepWorktree) {
    print(`E2: removing worktree ${sess.name} …`);
    try { cmdWorktreeRm([sess.name, '--repo', repoAbs, '--force']); }
    catch (e) { warn(`worktree rm reported: ${e.message}`); }
  } else if (sess.name) {
    print(`E2: keeping worktree ${sess.name} (--keep-worktree)`);
  }

  // The branch carried no content, so there is nothing for `handleShippedBranch` to reason about —
  // but it is also not "shipped", and deleting it here would destroy the only record that the
  // session existed. Left alone, and said so.
  print(`E2: branch ${sess.branch} left in place (it carried no content to ship; delete it by hand if you want it gone).`);

  if (skipped.length) {
    process.stderr.write(`✗ evidence-close finished with ${skipped.length} issue(s) still open (see above).\n`);
    return 1;
  }
  print(`✓ evidence-close complete: ${closed.map((n) => '#' + n).join(' ') || '(nothing to close)'}`);
  return 0;
}

function cmdShip(argv) {
  const opts = parseArgs(argv, {
    values: { '--repo': 'repo', '--worktree': 'worktree', '--branch': 'branch', '--message': 'message', '--refs': 'refs' },
    flags: { '--keep-worktree': 'keepWorktree', '--delete-branch': 'deleteBranch', '--dry': 'dry', '--json': 'json', '-h': 'help', '--help': 'help' },
  });
  if (opts.help) return print(HELP_SHIP);
  // --json is scoped to --dry only (#77): a machine-readable READ of the preconditions. Reporting
  // the PERFORMED result of a real ship as JSON is a different, not-yet-built feature (see HELP_SHIP)
  // — refuse the combination rather than silently ignore --json on a real ship.
  if (opts.json && !opts.dry) throw new UserError('--json currently only applies together with --dry — see `colab ship --help`');
  // A fully separate code path, deliberately: the prose/table path below is unchanged by this
  // feature (CONVENTIONS/#77 requires it stay byte-identical), so --dry --json does its own
  // resolution rather than threading a json branch through the existing one.
  if (opts.dry && opts.json) return cmdShipDryJson(opts);
  const repoAbs = resolveRepo(opts);
  if (!git.repoRoot(repoAbs)) throw new UserError(`${repoAbs} is not a git repo`);
  const doc = projectDoc(repoAbs);
  const st = state.loadState();
  const sess = resolveShipSession(st, repoAbs, opts);
  const trunk = resolveTrunk(repoAbs, doc);
  if (!trunk) throw new UserError('Could not determine trunk branch (set project.yml trunk:)');
  if (sess.branch === trunk) throw new UserError(`Refusing: --branch is the trunk itself (${trunk}) — nothing to ship`);

  // ---- the recorded branch must RESOLVE, before anything else is computed ----
  // Everything downstream is keyed to this name: the claim lookup above, the migration diff, the
  // squash. A worktree record holding a role word or a deleted branch fails all three quietly —
  // the claim set comes back empty and the merge lands with no `Closes #N` (#53).
  if (!git.branchExists(repoAbs, sess.branch)) {
    const shown = sess.branch === null || sess.branch === undefined ? 'null (no branch)' : `"${sess.branch}"`;
    process.stderr.write(`✗ Refusing: the branch recorded for this session is ${shown} — it resolves to no ref.\n`);
    if (sess.name) {
      process.stderr.write(`  Worktree "${sess.name}" cannot be shipped from a record nothing can act on. Repair it\n`);
      process.stderr.write('  through the CLI (never by editing ~/.colab/state.json — other sessions write to it):\n');
      process.stderr.write(`    colab doctor            # says what is broken, --prune repairs what it can prove\n`);
      process.stderr.write(`    colab release <N> && colab claim <N> --branch <real-branch> --worktree ${sess.name}\n`);
    } else {
      process.stderr.write(`  Pass a branch that exists, or fetch it first.\n`);
    }
    return 1;
  }

  // ---- the merge TARGET is the base the session was cut from, not trunk ----
  const integration = resolveIntegration(repoAbs, doc, trunk);
  // A declared line is never ship's cargo. Merging weeks of divergence into the branch that
  // promotes is an integration event of the same weight as a promotion, and `autonomy: auto-trunk`
  // grants no part of it — that field is about ordinary session branches.
  if (integration.includes(sess.branch)) {
    process.stderr.write(`✗ Refusing to ship "${sess.branch}" — it is a declared integration line (project.yml integration:).\n`);
    process.stderr.write(`  Merging a long-lived line into ${trunk} is a human act: it meets the branch that promotes, and\n`);
    process.stderr.write('  autonomy: auto-trunk does not cover it. Do it by hand, with COLAB_HUMAN=1 for the push.\n');
    return 1;
  }
  const target = resolveBase(st, repoAbs, sess.name, trunk);
  if (target !== trunk && !integration.includes(target)) {
    // The worktree records a base the repo no longer declares (the line was merged and dropped, or
    // project.yml was edited mid-session). Fail closed: silently retargeting at trunk is the exact
    // accident the recorded base exists to prevent.
    throw new UserError(
      `Worktree "${sess.name}" was cut from "${target}", which is no longer a declared base ` +
      `(allowed: ${allowedBases(repoAbs, doc, trunk).join(', ')}). Re-declare it in project.yml ` +
      '`integration:` or finish this merge by hand — ship will not retarget it at trunk for you.');
  }

  // ---- (a) AUTONOMY GATE — granted by the repo file, never by the caller ----
  const autonomy = doc.autonomy ? String(doc.autonomy) : 'manual';
  if (autonomy !== 'auto-trunk') {
    process.stderr.write(`✗ ${shortRepo(repoAbs)} does not grant auto-trunk (project.yml autonomy: ${doc.autonomy ? autonomy : 'absent'}).\n`);
    process.stderr.write('  A human must trigger Phase B. (Autonomy is granted by the repo, not by a flag — --force cannot override.)\n');
    return 1;
  }

  // ---- (b) PRECONDITIONS (report each ✓/✗) ----
  const gh = isGhUsable(repoAbs);
  const checks = [];

  // Which claimed issues are REFERENCED (Refs #N), not closed — three sources unioned by
  // resolveCloseRefsSplit (#48, #74): --refs, the tracking label, and #74's close gate (an
  // unticked ## Plan checklist with no declared Remainder: #M). The claim is still released
  // unconditionally either way (B3, unchanged) — only Closes→Refs changes.
  const refsFromFlag = new Set(
    String(opts.refs || '').split(',').map((s) => s.trim().replace(/^#/, '')).filter(Boolean).map(Number),
  );
  for (const n of refsFromFlag) {
    if (!sess.issues.includes(n)) warn(`--refs #${n} is not a claimed issue on this branch — ignored`);
  }
  const { closeIssues, refsIssues, refsSet, gateFindings } = resolveCloseRefsSplit(repoAbs, gh, sess.issues, refsFromFlag);
  // #74: a checklist-gated issue is a SURPRISE the operator did not ask for (unlike --refs or a
  // tracking label, both deliberate) — call it out on its own, loud, not folded silently into the
  // "issues:" line's "(ref — kept open)" annotation below.
  for (const f of gateFindings) {
    warn(`#${f.issue}: ${f.reason} — Closes withheld, Refs #${f.issue} written instead (does not block this ship).`);
    warn(`  Tick what shipped with evidence, file \`Remainder: #M\` for the rest, then re-run to close it.`);
  }
  // CI alive + green on the TARGET.
  //
  // On a declared line with NO runs at all, fall back to trunk's and say so. An ungated line is a
  // normal early state (the audit warns rather than fails on it, project.schema.md), and blocking
  // every ship into a line nobody has wired CI for would make the declared axis useless — which
  // pushes the work back to the undeclared workarounds. A line that HAS runs and is red still ✗:
  // once CI exists, it counts.
  let ciOk = false;
  const ciLabel = `${target === trunk ? 'trunk' : 'line'} CI green`;
  if (!gh) { checks.push(['✗', ciLabel, 'gh not usable (no auth / no origin) — cannot confirm CI']); }
  else {
    let run = git.ghRunForSha(repoAbs, target);
    let via = target;
    if (run && run.status === 'none' && target !== trunk) {
      run = git.ghRunForSha(repoAbs, trunk);
      via = `${trunk} (line "${target}" has no runs — ungated, advisory)`;
    }
    if (!run) checks.push(['✗', ciLabel, 'gh run list failed']);
    else if (run.status === 'completed' && run.conclusion === 'success') { ciOk = true; checks.push(['✓', ciLabel, `run for ${via}@${shortSha(run.sha)}: success`]); }
    else checks.push(['✗', ciLabel, `${via}@${shortSha(run.sha)}: status=${run.status} conclusion=${run.conclusion || 'none'} (fail-to-start counts as not green; a cancelled sibling of a passing run on this sha would read success, not this)`]);
  }
  // No new migrations on the branch, measured against what it will merge into — UNLESS every
  // issue the branch carries holds a live, branch-bound migration grant (#98). Grants are read
  // from the tracker only when a migration actually exists, so the ordinary ship pays nothing.
  const migrations = newMigrations(repoAbs, target, sess.branch);
  const migGrants = migrations.length ? shipMigrationGrants(repoAbs, gh, sess.issues, sess.branch) : null;
  const migOk = migrations.length === 0 || migGrants.ok;
  checks.push([migOk ? '✓' : '✗', 'no new migrations', migDetail(migrations, migGrants, target, sess.branch).text]);

  // ---- B1b's cross-check, run by the tool rather than by the reader (#87) ----
  // The claim registry is read AT SHIP TIME, so a claim a co-tenant session wrote onto this branch
  // minutes ago is indistinguishable from one this session made. Ask git whether the branch
  // corroborates each issue that is about to be CLOSED — an issue kept open by --refs/tracking/the
  // close gate was never going to close, so gating it a second way is pure noise.
  const shipCommits = branchCommits(repoAbs, target, sess.branch);
  const corr = shipguard.corroborateIssues(closeIssues, sess.branch, shipCommits);
  checks.push([corr.ok ? '✓' : '✗', 'claims corroborated by git',
    corr.ok
      ? (closeIssues.length ? `${closeIssues.length} closing issue(s) named by the branch or its commits` : 'none to close')
      : `${corr.uncorroborated.map((u) => '#' + u.issue).join(' ')} claimed but named by NEITHER the branch name nor any commit body`]);
  // The main checkout is on trunk and clean. Required whatever the target is: it is the repo's
  // at-rest invariant, and when the target is a line the merge happens in an ephemeral worktree
  // precisely so that this stays true.
  const headBranch = git.git(['rev-parse', '--abbrev-ref', 'HEAD'], repoAbs).stdout;
  const onTrunk = headBranch === trunk;
  const dirty = git.dirtyTracked(repoAbs); // tracked-only on purpose — see the #86 note in cmdShipJson
  const treeOk = onTrunk && !dirty;
  checks.push([treeOk ? '✓' : '✗', 'trunk checkout ready',
    !onTrunk ? `main checkout is on "${headBranch}", not "${trunk}"` : (dirty ? 'tracked changes uncommitted in trunk checkout' : `on ${trunk}, clean`)]);

  // #87 gates the ship: writing `Closes #N` for work that has not started is unrecoverable — the
  // issue can be reopened, but the false claim stays in trunk's immutable history, and GitHub will
  // have closed it the instant trunk was pushed.
  const preOk = ciOk && migOk && treeOk && corr.ok;

  // ---- report the table ----
  print(`ship ${shortRepo(repoAbs)} · branch ${sess.branch} → ${target}` +
    (target === trunk ? '' : `  [declared line — NOT ${trunk}]`) + (opts.dry ? '  [DRY RUN]' : ''));
  print(`  issues: ${sess.issues.map((n) => '#' + n + (refsSet.has(n) ? ' (ref — kept open)' : '')).join(' ') || '(none claimed)'}` + (sess.name ? ` · worktree ${sess.name}` : ''));

  // ---- an empty claim set is SUSPICIOUS, not benign (#53) ----
  // No claims means no `Closes #N`, which means every issue this branch fixes stays open with its
  // code merged — the failure CONVENTIONS.md §4 measured at 26/30 issues. It was the quiet path:
  // ship printed "(none claimed)" and carried on. It is now loud, and where the repo holds evidence
  // that the claim lookup itself is broken — a claim naming a branch that resolves to nothing — it
  // refuses, because then "no claims" is a lookup failure rather than a fact about the work.
  const brokenClaims = Object.values(st.claims).filter((c) => c.repo === repoAbs &&
    (records.branchProblem(c.branch) || (c.branch && !git.branchExists(repoAbs, c.branch))));
  // The remedy a human/agent is told to run must be the WORKTREE-keyed form whenever ship already
  // knows the worktree name (printed one line above, "· worktree <name>") — a claim written without
  // --worktree used to be invisible to ship even after following this exact remedy (#76): `ship`
  // resolves issues through name+branch now (the union above), but the shorter --branch-only form is
  // still the one that used to silently do nothing, so don't keep telling people to run it.
  const claimRemedy = sess.name
    ? `colab claim <N> --worktree ${sess.name} --branch ${sess.branch}`
    : `colab claim <N> --branch ${sess.branch}`;
  if (sess.issues.length === 0) {
    warn('this branch resolves to ZERO claimed issues — the squash will carry NO `Closes #N`,');
    warn('  so anything it fixes stays open with its code already merged (CONVENTIONS.md §4).');
    if (brokenClaims.length) {
      process.stderr.write(`✗ Refusing: ${brokenClaims.length} claim(s) in this repo name a branch that resolves to no ref,\n`);
      for (const c of brokenClaims) {
        process.stderr.write(`    ${c.issue}  branch ${c.branch === null ? 'null' : `"${c.branch}"`}${c.worktree ? ` · worktree ${c.worktree}` : ''}\n`);
      }
      process.stderr.write('  so "no claims" here is a broken lookup, not a fact about this branch. Repair, then re-run:\n');
      process.stderr.write(`    colab release <N> && ${claimRemedy}\n`);
      process.stderr.write('  (through the CLI — several sessions write ~/.colab/state.json, so a hand edit can lose one.)\n');
      return 1;
    }
    warn('  Nothing contradicts it, so ship continues. If this branch was meant to close an issue,');
    warn(`  stop now and:  ${claimRemedy}`);
  } else if (brokenClaims.length) {
    warn(`${brokenClaims.length} claim(s) in this repo name a branch that resolves to no ref — run \`colab doctor\``);
  }

  // ---- #87: say what an uncorroborated claim means, and what to do about it ----
  // Printed as a refusal rather than a warning because the failure is not recoverable after the
  // fact: the issue can be reopened, but `Closes #N` stays in trunk's history forever.
  if (!corr.ok) {
    for (const u of corr.uncorroborated) {
      warn(`#${u.issue} is claimed on this branch but NOTHING in git corroborates it — not the branch`);
      warn(`  name's trailing group, not any commit body. The usual cause is a co-tenant session that`);
      warn(`  claimed it onto this branch/worktree while this ship was being prepared; its work may not`);
      warn(`  have started. Shipping would write "Closes #${u.issue}" into an immutable commit and close it.`);
    }
    warn('  Resolve by deciding what is true, then re-run:');
    warn(`    colab release <N>                 # the claim is not this branch's work`);
    warn(`    colab ship … --refs <N>           # it IS related, but this ship does not complete it`);
    warn(`    (or land a commit whose body names #N, if the branch really does implement it)`);
  }

  // ---- #88 Case 1: does the inherited subject describe the unit being shipped? ----
  // Both signals are advisory: they are printed before the irreversible step and never block, in
  // line with #88's own ruling ("neither should block a ship on its own; both should be visible
  // before the irreversible step"). `--message` replaces the subject outright, so neither applies.
  if (!opts.message && shipCommits.length) {
    const idx = squash.pickSubjectIndex(shipCommits);
    if (idx >= 0) {
      const chosen = shipCommits[idx];
      const findings = shipguard.subjectSanity({
        subject: chosen.subject,
        branchName: sess.branch,
        chosenFiles: commitFiles(repoAbs, chosen.sha),
        branchFiles: branchChangedFiles(repoAbs, target, sess.branch),
        commitCount: shipCommits.length,
        parseSubject: squash.parseSubject,
      });
      for (const f of findings) {
        warn(`squash subject (${f.kind}): ${f.detail}`);
        warn(`  chosen from ${String(chosen.sha).slice(0, 7)} "${chosen.subject}" — pass --message "<subject>" to title it by hand.`);
      }
    }
  }

  // ---- surface the squash-subject blind spot BEFORE it ships (#88) ----
  // A commit whose prefix isn't a recognised Conventional Commit type (`wip:`, or no prefix at
  // all) can never win the squash subject on merit — pickSubjectIndex cannot see it, so a
  // LOWER-WEIGHT recognised commit (often the closing `docs:` pass) wins instead, silently. Only
  // meaningful on the composed path: `--message` replaces the subject outright.
  if (!opts.message) {
    const unweighted = squash.unweightedCommits(shipCommits);
    if (unweighted.length) {
      warn(`${unweighted.length} commit(s) on this branch carry no recognised Conventional Commit type`);
      warn('  — invisible to the squash-subject ranking, however small the commit that wins instead:');
      for (const c of unweighted) warn(`    ${String(c.sha || '').slice(0, 7)}  ${c.subject}`);
      warn('  If one of these is the branch\'s real headline, re-word it to a recognised type, or ship');
      warn('  with --message so the subject is not guessed.');
    }
  }
  // ---- (b2) which completion path is this? squash-merge, or evidence-close (#90) ----
  // A session can finish with a real deliverable and ZERO commits: a decision recorded on its
  // issue, an investigation concluding "no change needed", a design artifact stored outside the
  // repo. That branch classifies as `landed` (nothing to merge), so B1 used to refuse it AFTER
  // B0's sync, the CI check and the push gate — and no path in either skill ever closed the issue,
  // because the only close mechanism in the whole system was `Closes #N` inside a squash commit.
  // The claim got released, the worktree torn down, and the issue stayed open forever.
  //
  // The zero-diff fact is measured from git, never declared by the session.
  const mode = shipMode(repoAbs, target, sess.branch, shipCommits);

  print('');
  print(table(checks.map((c) => [c[0], c[1], c[2]]), ['', 'PRECONDITION', 'DETAIL']));
  print('');

  if (mode.evidenceClose) {
    print('→ MODE: evidence-close (the branch introduces no change to ' + target + ' — nothing to merge)');
    print(`  ${mode.why}`);
  }

  if (opts.dry) {
    print(preOk ? '→ READY: preconditions pass. Re-run without --dry to ship.' : '→ NOT READY: fix the ✗ rows above.');
    if (mode.evidenceClose) {
      print('  Plan: no merge, no commit, no push · post evidence comment + CLOSE each issue · ' +
        (opts.keepWorktree ? 'keep worktree' : 'worktree rm') + ' (releases claims)');
      print('  Gate: every closing issue must already carry an evidence comment — checked at run time.');
    } else {
    print(`  Plan: B0 sync ${target}→branch · B1 squash-merge → ` + target + ' · B2 push · B3 ' +
      (opts.keepWorktree ? 'keep worktree' : 'worktree rm') + ' + ' +
      (opts.deleteBranch && !opts.keepWorktree ? 'delete branch (local+remote)' : 'keep branch') +
      ' · B4 verify closed · post 🚢 summary');
    }
    // Show the subject the squash WOULD get. The whole point of issue #17 was that a wrong subject
    // is silent — nothing fails, CI is green, and it is only visible once it is in a published tag.
    // A dry run is the last moment it can be caught, so it must be on screen there.
    //
    // An empty default (#57) means the branch has no commits of its own to derive a subject from —
    // say that plainly rather than printing a blank line that reads like a rendering glitch.
    if (!mode.evidenceClose) {
      let subjectLine;
      if (opts.message) {
        subjectLine = `${opts.message} [--message]`;
      } else {
        const built = buildSquashMessage(repoAbs, target, sess.branch, closeIssues, refsIssues);
        subjectLine = built ? built.split('\n')[0] : '(none — branch has no commits of its own; pass --message)';
      }
      print('  Squash subject: ' + subjectLine);
    }
    if (refsIssues.length) print(`  Refs (kept open, NOT closed): ${refsIssues.map((n) => '#' + n).join(' ')}`);
    return preOk ? 0 : 1;
  }
  if (!preOk) { process.stderr.write('✗ Aborting: one or more preconditions failed (see ✗ above). Nothing was pushed.\n'); return 1; }

  // ---- (b3) evidence-close: the whole B0–B2 sequence is skipped, because there is nothing to merge ----
  if (mode.evidenceClose) return shipEvidenceClose({
    repoAbs, st, sess, target, trunk, gh, closeIssues, refsIssues, refsSet, mode, opts,
  });

  // ---- (c) B0: merge the TARGET into the branch (in the worktree, or an ephemeral one) ----
  const generated = [...DEFAULT_GENERATED, ...(Array.isArray(doc.generated) ? doc.generated.map(String) : [])];
  const hasPreShip = fs.existsSync(path.join(repoAbs, '.colab', 'hooks', 'pre-ship'));
  let branchDir = sess.wtPath && fs.existsSync(sess.wtPath) ? sess.wtPath : null;
  let ephemeral = false;
  if (!branchDir) {
    branchDir = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'colab-ship-'));
    const add = git.git(['worktree', 'add', branchDir, sess.branch], repoAbs);
    if (!add.ok) { try { fs.rmdirSync(branchDir); } catch (_) {} throw new UserError(`could not check out ${sess.branch} for B0: ${add.stderr.split('\n')[0]}`); }
    ephemeral = true;
  }
  const cleanupEphemeral = () => { if (ephemeral) git.git(['worktree', 'remove', '--force', branchDir], repoAbs); };

  print(`B0: syncing ${target} into the branch …`);
  git.git(['fetch', 'origin', target], branchDir);
  let mergeRef = `origin/${target}`;
  if (!git.git(['rev-parse', '--verify', '--quiet', mergeRef], branchDir).ok) mergeRef = target;
  const merge = git.git(['merge', '--no-edit', mergeRef], branchDir);
  if (!merge.ok) {
    const uf = git.git(['diff', '--name-only', '--diff-filter=U'], branchDir).stdout.split('\n').filter(Boolean);
    // An empty unmerged-path list does NOT mean "no conflicts" here — it means git never entered a
    // conflicted merge state at all. The common cause is uncommitted local changes to a file the merge
    // would touch ("Your local changes … would be overwritten by merge. Aborting"), refused before the
    // merge machinery starts. Every branch below this point reasons off `uf`/`nonGen`, so a refused
    // merge used to fall through to the generated-file path and print an empty, actionable-nothing list
    // under the word "conflicts" — wrong noun, empty detail (#60). Distinguish it before branching.
    if (uf.length === 0) {
      cleanupEphemeral();
      process.stderr.write('✗ B0: merge refused before it started — not a conflict:\n' +
        (merge.stderr ? merge.stderr.split('\n').map((l) => `  ${l}`).join('\n') + '\n' : '  (git gave no message)\n'));
      return 1;
    }
    const nonGen = uf.filter((f) => !isGeneratedPath(f, generated));
    if (nonGen.length) {
      git.git(['merge', '--abort'], branchDir); cleanupEphemeral();
      process.stderr.write(`✗ B0: merge conflicts in NON-generated files — hand-merge needed, human Phase B:\n  ${nonGen.join('\n  ')}\n`);
      return 1;
    }
    if (hasPreShip) {
      const hk = runHook(repoAbs, 'pre-ship', { wtPath: branchDir, name: sess.name || sess.branch, branch: sess.branch, ports: [], trunk, base: target, issues: sess.issues });
      const committed = hk.ran && hk.ok && git.git(['commit', '--no-edit'], branchDir).ok;
      if (!committed) {
        git.git(['merge', '--abort'], branchDir); cleanupEphemeral();
        process.stderr.write('✗ B0: generated-file conflicts and pre-ship hook could not resolve them. Nothing pushed.\n');
        return 1;
      }
      print(`  generated-file conflicts resolved by .colab/hooks/pre-ship (${uf.join(', ')})`);
    } else {
      git.git(['merge', '--abort'], branchDir); cleanupEphemeral();
      process.stderr.write(`✗ B0: generated-file conflicts and no .colab/hooks/pre-ship to regenerate:\n  ${uf.join('\n  ')}\n`);
      return 1;
    }
  } else {
    print(`  branch is in sync with ${target}.`);
  }

  // ---- (d) B1: re-verify CI, then squash-merge into the target ----
  if (gh) {
    let run2 = git.ghRunForSha(repoAbs, target);
    // Same fallback as the precondition: an ungated line borrows trunk's verdict rather than
    // blocking. The two must agree, or a ship passes the table and dies at the re-check.
    if (run2 && run2.status === 'none' && target !== trunk) run2 = git.ghRunForSha(repoAbs, trunk);
    if (!(run2 && run2.status === 'completed' && run2.conclusion === 'success')) {
      cleanupEphemeral();
      process.stderr.write(`✗ B1: CI for ${target} is no longer green — aborting before merge. Nothing pushed.\n`);
      return 1;
    }
  }
  // WHERE the squash happens. Into trunk it is the main checkout, which is on trunk by invariant
  // (and was just verified to be). Into a declared line it must NOT be: checking the main checkout
  // out onto a long-lived line is the very state that made this axis necessary — everything reading
  // that working tree would serve the line, and a merge is not a reason to break the invariant for
  // even a moment. So the line is merged in an ephemeral worktree that nothing else reads.
  let targetDir = repoAbs;
  let ephemeralTarget = false;
  if (target !== trunk) {
    targetDir = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'colab-ship-target-'));
    const addT = git.git(['worktree', 'add', targetDir, target], repoAbs);
    if (!addT.ok) {
      cleanupEphemeral();
      try { fs.rmdirSync(targetDir); } catch (_) {}
      process.stderr.write(`✗ B1: could not check out "${target}" to merge into: ${addT.stderr.split('\n')[0]}. Nothing pushed.\n`);
      return 1;
    }
    ephemeralTarget = true;
  }
  const cleanupTarget = () => { if (ephemeralTarget) git.git(['worktree', 'remove', '--force', targetDir], repoAbs); };

  let commitMsg;
  // Collects one {num, from, to} per inherited trailer the composer overruled (#58: a branch commit
  // wrote e.g. `Refs #53` before #53 was decided as a Closes) — surfaced below, not silently dropped.
  const trailerConflicts = [];
  if (opts.message) {
    // --message overrides the subject/body, but Closes is spliced in as its own paragraph — never
    // concatenated. Appending it welded ` — Closes #N` onto whatever the message ended with, which
    // for any message carrying a trailer block corrupted the last trailer's value.
    commitMsg = squash.spliceCloses(opts.message, closeIssues, refsIssues, trailerConflicts);
  } else {
    // Preserve the branch's crafted commit (subject + footers); insert Closes/Refs above the footers.
    commitMsg = buildSquashMessage(repoAbs, target, sess.branch, closeIssues, refsIssues, trailerConflicts);
    if (!commitMsg) {
      // #57: no commit on the branch's own first-parent chain carries a subject to borrow. Failing
      // here, with a clear reason, beats either of the two silent alternatives — inventing one from
      // the base's tip (the original bug) or letting `git commit -m ''` reject it with a generic
      // "empty commit message" that does not say WHY. composeSquashMessage returns '' before ever
      // touching spliceCloses/reconcileClosesRefsConflict for an empty commit list, so trailerConflicts
      // is guaranteed empty here too — nothing for the warn loop below to report on this path.
      cleanupEphemeral(); cleanupTarget();
      process.stderr.write('✗ B1: the branch has no commits of its own to derive a squash subject from — ' +
        're-run with --message "<subject>" to supply one by hand. Nothing pushed.\n');
      return 1;
    }
  }
  for (const cf of trailerConflicts) {
    warn(`#${cf.num}: the branch commit said "${cf.from} #${cf.num}" but this ship closes it (${cf.to} #${cf.num}) — inherited trailer dropped, kept ship's own.`);
  }

  print(`B1: squash-merging ${sess.branch} → ${target} …`);
  const sq = git.git(['merge', '--squash', sess.branch], targetDir);
  if (!sq.ok) {
    git.git(['merge', '--abort'], targetDir); cleanupEphemeral(); cleanupTarget();
    process.stderr.write(`✗ B1: squash-merge failed: ${sq.stderr.split('\n')[0]}. Nothing pushed.\n`);
    return 1;
  }
  const commit = git.run('git', ['commit', '-m', commitMsg], { cwd: targetDir });
  if (!commit.ok) {
    git.git(['reset', '--hard', `origin/${target}`], targetDir); cleanupEphemeral(); cleanupTarget();
    // if there was genuinely nothing to commit, say so plainly
    process.stderr.write(`✗ B1: commit failed (${commit.stderr.split('\n')[0] || 'nothing to commit?'}). ${target} reset, nothing pushed.\n`);
    return 1;
  }
  const sha = git.git(['rev-parse', '--short', 'HEAD'], targetDir).stdout;
  print(`  committed ${sha}: ${commitMsg}`);

  // B1 has landed the squash on the target → the worktree's branch is now merged. Record it before B3,
  // so a --keep-worktree entry survives with status=merged (and doctor treats it as a sweep candidate).
  if (sess.name) state.mutate((st) => { if (st.worktrees[sess.name]) st.worktrees[sess.name].status = 'merged'; });

  // ---- (e) B2: push the target with COLAB_SHIP=1 (the sanctioned-path signal for pre-push-guard) ----
  print(`B2: pushing ${target} …`);
  const push = git.run('git', ['push', 'origin', target], { cwd: targetDir, env: { ...process.env, COLAB_SHIP: '1' } });
  if (!push.ok) {
    process.stderr.write(`✗ B2: push failed: ${push.stderr.split('\n')[0]}. ${target} committed locally at ${sha} but NOT pushed — resolve and push by hand (COLAB_HUMAN=1).\n`);
    cleanupEphemeral(); cleanupTarget();
    return 1;
  }
  print('  pushed.');
  cleanupEphemeral();
  cleanupTarget();

  // The work has landed and is pushed — this is the moment worth reporting, and it is reported HERE
  // rather than at the end of the command so it precedes the teardown events B3 emits. Ship is a
  // worktree state change, not a claim change: the claims are released by B3, which speaks for them.
  pushEvent('ship', {
    repo: repoAbs, worktree: sess.name, session: sess.session,
    issue: sess.issues.length === 1 ? sess.issues[0] : undefined,
    payload: { state: 'merged', branch: sess.branch, target, sha, issues: sess.issues },
  });

  // Then, PER ISSUE, a merge-completion event (#46 §2, following #45 §2). The ship event above is
  // worktree-scoped — for a group it carries every issue in one payload — but the receiver flips a
  // single issue to done the instant its merge lands, without waiting for the eventually-consistent
  // read. One-per-issue (not a fan-out field) was the agreed shape: kind `issue.merged`, the merge
  // commit as evidence. repo and issue ride the envelope like every other event; the sha is the only
  // per-event delta, so it alone is the payload. A dropped one costs the provider's read-after-write
  // lag, same class as readiness — best-effort, never load-bearing.
  //
  // Only for CLOSED issues (#48): `issue.merged` tells the receiver to flip an issue to done, which
  // is exactly wrong for a tracking issue the branch merely referenced — its work is not complete.
  for (const num of closeIssues) pushEvent('issue-merged', { repo: repoAbs, issue: num, payload: { sha } });

  // ---- (f) B3: teardown the worktree (releases claims + ports + ✅ comments) ----
  const worktreeKept = !!(sess.name && opts.keepWorktree);
  if (sess.name && !opts.keepWorktree) {
    print(`B3: removing worktree ${sess.name} …`);
    try { cmdWorktreeRm([sess.name, '--repo', repoAbs, '--force']); }
    catch (e) { warn(`worktree rm reported: ${e.message}`); }
  } else if (sess.name) {
    print(`B3: keeping worktree ${sess.name} (--keep-worktree)`);
  }

  // B3 (cont.) — the shipped branch. Kept by default; --delete-branch opts in. MUST come after the
  // worktree removal above: git refuses to delete a branch checked out in a worktree.
  handleShippedBranch(repoAbs, target, sess.branch, { deleteBranch: opts.deleteBranch, worktreeKept, integration });

  // ---- (g/h) B4 verify + post summary per issue ----
  const groupLabelsCarried = new Set(); // union across every issue this branch carried — for (i) below
  if (gh) {
    for (const num of sess.issues) {
      const isRef = refsSet.has(num);
      const info = git.ghIssueView(repoAbs, num, ['state', 'labels']);
      // A closed issue must have auto-closed; a ref'd (tracking) issue must have STAYED open. The
      // second check catches the one case the pure layer cannot fix: a carried commit body that
      // literally says `Closes #N` for a ref issue — GitHub honours it and closes the memory issue.
      if (!isRef && info && info.state !== 'CLOSED') warn(`#${num} did not auto-close (state=${info.state}) — the Closes trailer needs a push to the DEFAULT branch; close it by hand if ${target} isn't default`);
      if (isRef && info && info.state === 'CLOSED') warn(`#${num} was meant to stay open (Refs, tracking issue) but is CLOSED — a commit body likely carries "Closes #${num}"; reopen it by hand`);
      for (const gl of labels.groupLabelNames(info && info.labels)) groupLabelsCarried.add(gl);
      const shipSess = sessionCommentSuffix(sess.session, sess.sessionName); // markdown link when both present
      // Name the branch it actually landed on. An issue whose code is on a long-lived line is NOT
      // in trunk, and a comment saying otherwise would be read as "this is in the next release".
      const onLine = target === trunk ? '' : ` (a declared integration line — NOT yet in ${trunk})`;
      const body = isRef
        ? `🔖 Referenced by colab ship (kept open — tracking issue) — ${sha}${onLine}${shipSess}`
        : `🚢 Shipped to ${target} by colab ship — ${sha}${onLine}${shipSess}`;
      const r = git.ghIssueComment(repoAbs, num, body);
      if (!r.ok) warn(`could not post ship comment on #${num}`);
    }
  }

  // ---- (i) group-label teardown (#82) ----
  // A `group:<key>` label (CONVENTIONS.md §5, Grouping) records that a set of issues had to
  // share one branch — it stops being useful the moment every member is closed, and left in
  // place it is picker noise forever (measured: ~12 stale entries on one fleet repo). Only the
  // issues THIS branch carried can trigger a check — that is the set whose state just changed —
  // but membership is read fresh per label, because a group can outlive the branch that closed
  // its last member.
  if (gh) {
    for (const gl of groupLabelsCarried) {
      const openMembers = git.ghIssueListByLabel(repoAbs, gl, 'open', ['number']);
      if (openMembers === null) { warn(`could not check membership of ${gl} — left in place`); continue; }
      if (openMembers.length > 0) continue; // still binds the remainder — leave it
      const del = git.ghLabelDelete(repoAbs, gl);
      if (del.ok) print(`  deleted spent group label ${gl} (every member now closed)`);
      else warn(`could not delete spent group label ${gl}: ${del.stderr || 'gh label delete failed'}`);
    }
  }

  print('');
  print(`✓ Shipped ${sess.branch} → ${target} @ ${sha}${sess.issues.length ? ' (' + sess.issues.map((n) => '#' + n).join(' ') + ')' : ''}`);
  if (target !== trunk) print(`  "${target}" is a declared line: this work is NOT in ${trunk} until a human merges the line.`);
  return 0;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// landed — "has this branch's work landed on its base?", the one shared answer.
//
// code-sweep sorts candidates into wrap / teardown-only / claim-only / blocked / unlinked, and code-wrap
// Phase B decides the same thing for one branch. Both used to decide it by prose and judgement,
// and the obvious rule (count the commits ahead) misreports every squash-merge — see lib/landed.js
// for the measurement and for exactly what the rule does and does not cover.
//
// It reads; it never acts. Teardown stays a separate, deliberate command, because a rule that both
// decides and deletes is one bug away from deleting on an `unknown`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

const HELP_LANDED = `colab landed [--repo <path>] [--worktree <name> | --branch <br>] [--base <br>] [--all] [--json]

Has this branch's work already landed on its base? The shared answer behind code-sweep's buckets
and code-wrap Phase B, so the two stop deciding it by eye.

  landed    the base already contains this content — teardown, do NOT re-merge
  cargo     genuine unmerged work — a full wrap
  unknown   no confident answer — treat as cargo and look by hand (never torn down)

The base is the branch's RECORDED base (\`colab worktree new --base\`), else the repo's trunk from
.github/project.yml. It is never defaulted to "main". --base overrides for an ad-hoc question.

Why "commits ahead" is not the rule: a squash-merge mints a new sha, so a shipped branch's own
commits are never ancestors of the base and a count-only test calls every branch we have ever
shipped unfinished. The mirror rule (diff only) fails when the base moved on underneath. Both
failures were measured on real worktrees. So the answer comes from content — does merging this
branch change the base's tree at all — which stays correct across squashes and later base movement.

LIMIT, stated rather than rounded up: if the base has REWRITTEN the branch's work, the merge
conflicts and no content answer exists. That reports \`unknown\`, never \`landed\`.

  --worktree <name>   ask about a worktree's branch, using its recorded base
  --branch <br>       ask about a branch by name
  --all               every worktree of this repo, as a table
  --base <br>         compare against this ref instead
  --json              machine-readable
No network: fetch first if the base ref may be stale.
`;

/** Trunk for `landed` — project.yml, then the LOCAL origin/HEAD ref. Never "main", never network. */
function landedBaseTrunk(repoAbs) {
  const doc = projectDoc(repoAbs);
  if (doc && doc.trunk) return String(doc.trunk);
  const head = git.git(['symbolic-ref', '--short', 'refs/remotes/origin/HEAD'], repoAbs);
  if (head.ok && head.stdout) return head.stdout.replace(/^origin\//, '');
  throw new UserError(
    'Could not determine the base: no .github/project.yml `trunk:` and no local origin/HEAD. ' +
    'Pass --base <branch>. (It is never assumed to be "main" — guessing the base is how a branch ' +
    'gets measured against the wrong one.)');
}

function cmdLanded(argv) {
  const opts = parseArgs(argv, {
    values: { '--repo': 'repo', '--worktree': 'worktree', '--branch': 'branch', '--base': 'base' },
    flags: { '--all': 'all', '--json': 'json', '-h': 'help', '--help': 'help' },
  });
  if (opts.help) return print(HELP_LANDED);
  const repoAbs = resolveRepo(opts);
  if (!git.repoRoot(repoAbs)) throw new UserError(`${repoAbs} is not a git repo`);
  const st = state.loadState();
  const trunk = opts.base ? String(opts.base) : landedBaseTrunk(repoAbs);

  const ask = (branch, wtName) => {
    const base = opts.base ? String(opts.base) : resolveBase(st, repoAbs, wtName, trunk);
    return { worktree: wtName || null, ...landed.landedState(repoAbs, base, branch) };
  };

  let rows;
  if (opts.all) {
    rows = Object.values(st.worktrees)
      .filter((w) => w.repo === repoAbs)
      .map((w) => ask(w.branch, w.name));
    if (!rows.length) { print('No worktrees for this repo.'); return 0; }
  } else if (opts.worktree) {
    const wt = st.worktrees[opts.worktree];
    if (!wt) throw new UserError(`No worktree "${opts.worktree}" in state`);
    rows = [ask(wt.branch, wt.name)];
  } else if (opts.branch) {
    const wt = Object.values(st.worktrees).find((w) => w.repo === repoAbs && w.branch === opts.branch);
    rows = [ask(opts.branch, wt ? wt.name : null)];
  } else {
    throw new UserError('landed needs --worktree <name>, --branch <br>, or --all');
  }

  if (opts.json) { print(JSON.stringify(rows, null, 2)); return 0; }
  print(table(
    rows.map((r) => [r.branch, r.base, r.state, `${r.commitsAhead === null ? '?' : r.commitsAhead} ahead`, r.method, r.why]),
    ['BRANCH', 'BASE', 'VERDICT', 'COMMITS', 'BY', 'WHY'],
  ));
  // Exit code carries the answer for one branch, so a script can branch on it without parsing:
  // 0 = landed, 1 = cargo or unknown (i.e. "do not tear this down"). --all is informational only.
  if (opts.all) return 0;
  return landed.hasCargo(rows[0]) ? 1 : 0;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// promote — the checked door for PROMOTION (trunk → main), distinct from RELEASE (the tag).
//
// The ladder is: ship (branch → trunk) · promote (trunk → main) · release (tag, always human).
// Promotion permission is split from release permission on purpose: advancing main can be a heavy
// CI verification step (deploy: tag) that the main-loop may do unattended, OR a production deploy
// (deploy: push-main) that always needs a human. `promote` NEVER tags and NEVER deploys directly.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

const HELP_PROMOTE = `colab promote [--repo <path>] [--message "<msg>"] [--dry]

Promote the trunk into main (a --no-ff merge). The SECOND rung after \`ship\`: ship lands work on
trunk; promote advances main. It NEVER tags and NEVER deploys directly — RELEASE (the tag) stays a
human step. There is no --tag/--release flag; promote only ever merges trunk→main and pushes main.

Two gates, both must pass:
  • HARD SAFETY (from the repo's deploy semantics — no field or flag can lower it):
      deploy: push-main → promoting IS a production deploy → requires COLAB_HUMAN=1
      deploy: manual    → promoting is the human's "about to deploy" step → requires COLAB_HUMAN=1
      deploy: tag       → promotion is verification-only (heavy CI on main, no deploy)
      anything else / absent → treated as production-risk → requires COLAB_HUMAN=1 (fail-closed)
  • promotion field (project.yml \`promotion: human | main-loop\`, default human, fail-closed):
      on a deploy:tag repo, \`promotion: main-loop\` lets the main-loop promote with no human word;
      otherwise a human (COLAB_HUMAN=1) is required.
  Tier B (trunk == main) has NO promotion — ship goes straight to main; promote refuses.
  Tier C (deploy: push-main) promotes like A, but the promotion IS the deploy — always human.

On a deploy: manual repo a SUCCESSFUL promote leaves production untouched — main moves, nothing
deploys, and no workflow will fire. The command prints the repo's runbook: as the required next
step, because finishing it does not finish the job.

Preconditions (✓/✗): trunk CI green · trunk == origin/trunk · main == origin/main · main checkout usable.

Options:
  --repo <path>     repo root (default: git root of cwd)
  --message "<msg>" full override of the merge commit message (default: the release: line)
  --dry             print the precondition table + plan; change nothing
`;

// The post-promotion notice for `deploy: manual`, as an array of lines (pure — testable).
//
// On `tag` and `push-main` repos something downstream still runs: the tag deploys later, or
// the push just deployed. `manual` is the ONE case where a SUCCESSFUL command leaves the job
// unfinished — main moved, production did not, and nothing else will ever fire. Said quietly,
// that produces the exact failure this rung exists to prevent: a promoted main everyone reads
// as "shipped" while users are still on the old code. So it gets a block, not a line, and it
// names the runbook the operator has to go run.
function manualDeployBlock(runbookRaw, mainName) {
  const runbook = runbookRaw ? String(runbookRaw).trim() : '';
  const L = [
    '',
    '  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────',
    '  │ PRODUCTION IS NOT UPDATED — deploy: manual',
    '  │',
    `  │ ${mainName} now matches what you are ABOUT to put on the host.`,
    '  │ Nothing has reached production. No workflow will fire. Until a',
    `  │ human runs the deploy, ${mainName} is AHEAD of what users run.`,
    '  │',
  ];
  if (runbook) {
    L.push('  │ NEXT STEP — run this runbook by hand:', `  │     ${runbook}`);
  } else {
    // Never print an empty path: a blank line here would read as "no step required",
    // which is the one thing that must not happen on this branch.
    L.push(
      '  │ NEXT STEP — UNKNOWN: project.yml declares no runbook:.',
      '  │     The hand-deploy is not written down, so this command cannot',
      '  │     tell you what to run. Add runbook: <path> (the audit flags',
      '  │     its absence) and commit the procedure.',
    );
  }
  L.push(
    '  │',
    '  │ Then tag what you deployed — nothing fires from it, but it is',
    '  │ the record of what is on the host.',
    '  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────',
  );
  return L;
}

function cmdPromote(argv) {
  const opts = parseArgs(argv, {
    values: { '--repo': 'repo', '--message': 'message' },
    flags: { '--dry': 'dry', '-h': 'help', '--help': 'help' },
  });
  if (opts.help) return print(HELP_PROMOTE);
  const repoAbs = resolveRepo(opts);
  if (!git.repoRoot(repoAbs)) throw new UserError(`${repoAbs} is not a git repo`);
  const doc = projectDoc(repoAbs);
  const trunk = resolveTrunk(repoAbs, doc);
  if (!trunk) throw new UserError('Could not determine trunk branch (set project.yml trunk:)');
  const MAIN = 'main';

  // Tier B: trunk IS main → there is no promotion to run.
  if (trunk === MAIN) {
    process.stderr.write(`✗ ${shortRepo(repoAbs)}: trunk is "main" (Tier B) — no promotion exists on this repo. Ship goes straight to main.\n`);
    return 1;
  }

  const deploy = doc.deploy ? String(doc.deploy) : '';
  const promotion = doc.promotion === 'main-loop' ? 'main-loop' : 'human'; // fail-closed on unknown
  const human = process.env.COLAB_HUMAN === '1';
  const deployIsTag = deploy === 'tag';

  // ---- GATE (returns before any table, like ship's autonomy gate) ----
  if (deployIsTag) {
    // verification-only promotion: main-loop may run it, else a human must.
    if (promotion !== 'main-loop' && !human) {
      process.stderr.write(`✗ ${shortRepo(repoAbs)}: promotion=${doc.promotion ? promotion : 'human (default)'} — a human must promote ${trunk}→main.\n`);
      process.stderr.write('  Re-run with COLAB_HUMAN=1, or set project.yml promotion: main-loop (deploy is by tag, so promotion is verification-only).\n');
      return 1;
    }
  } else {
    // push-main (or unknown/absent) → promoting main is a production deploy → human required, always.
    if (!human) {
      const why = deploy === 'push-main'
        ? `deploy=push-main — promoting ${trunk}→main IS a PRODUCTION DEPLOY`
        : deploy === 'manual'
          ? `deploy=manual — promoting ${trunk}→main is the "about to deploy" step, and the hand-deploy that follows has no gate but the operator`
          : `deploy=${deploy || '(absent)'} — promoting ${trunk}→main may deploy to production (fail-closed)`;
      process.stderr.write(`✗ ${shortRepo(repoAbs)}: ${why}.\n`);
      process.stderr.write('  This requires a human: re-run with COLAB_HUMAN=1. (No field or flag can lower this bar.)\n');
      return 1;
    }
  }

  // ---- PRECONDITIONS (✓/✗) ----
  const gh = isGhUsable(repoAbs);
  const checks = [];
  git.git(['fetch', 'origin', trunk, MAIN], repoAbs); // best-effort: refresh both refs before comparing

  let ciOk = false;
  if (!gh) checks.push(['✗', 'trunk CI green', 'gh not usable (no auth / no origin) — cannot confirm CI']);
  else {
    const run = git.ghRunForSha(repoAbs, trunk);
    if (!run) checks.push(['✗', 'trunk CI green', 'gh run list failed']);
    else if (run.status === 'completed' && run.conclusion === 'success') { ciOk = true; checks.push(['✓', 'trunk CI green', `run for ${trunk}@${shortSha(run.sha)}: success`]); }
    else checks.push(['✗', 'trunk CI green', `${trunk}@${shortSha(run.sha)}: status=${run.status} conclusion=${run.conclusion || 'none'} (fail-to-start counts as not green)`]);
  }

  const localTrunk = git.git(['rev-parse', trunk], repoAbs);
  const originTrunk = git.git(['rev-parse', `origin/${trunk}`], repoAbs);
  const trunkSyncOk = localTrunk.ok && originTrunk.ok && localTrunk.stdout === originTrunk.stdout;
  checks.push([trunkSyncOk ? '✓' : '✗', `${trunk} == origin/${trunk}`, trunkSyncOk ? 'in sync' : 'local trunk differs from origin (push/pull first)']);

  const localMain = git.git(['rev-parse', MAIN], repoAbs);
  const originMain = git.git(['rev-parse', `origin/${MAIN}`], repoAbs);
  const mainSyncOk = localMain.ok && originMain.ok && localMain.stdout === originMain.stdout;
  checks.push([mainSyncOk ? '✓' : '✗', `${MAIN} == origin/${MAIN}`,
    !localMain.ok ? 'no local main branch (create it tracking origin/main)' : (mainSyncOk ? 'in sync' : 'local main differs from origin')]);

  // main checkout usable: reuse the repo checkout only if it is ON main and clean; else a temp worktree.
  const headBranch = git.git(['rev-parse', '--abbrev-ref', 'HEAD'], repoAbs).stdout;
  let useRepoDir = false, checkoutOk, checkoutDetail;
  if (headBranch === MAIN) {
    const dirty = git.dirtyTracked(repoAbs); // tracked-only on purpose — see the #86 note in cmdShipJson
    checkoutOk = !dirty; useRepoDir = !dirty;
    checkoutDetail = dirty ? 'main checkout has uncommitted tracked changes' : 'on main, clean';
  } else {
    checkoutOk = localMain.ok; // will use a temp worktree; needs a local main ref to check out
    checkoutDetail = localMain.ok ? `checkout on "${headBranch}" — will use a temp worktree for main` : 'no local main to check out';
  }
  checks.push([checkoutOk ? '✓' : '✗', 'main checkout usable', checkoutDetail]);

  const preOk = ciOk && trunkSyncOk && mainSyncOk && checkoutOk;

  // ---- report ----
  print(`promote ${shortRepo(repoAbs)} · ${trunk} → ${MAIN}` + (opts.dry ? '  [DRY RUN]' : ''));
  print(`  deploy: ${deploy || '(absent)'} · promotion: ${promotion}${human ? ' · COLAB_HUMAN=1' : ''}`);
  print('');
  print(table(checks.map((c) => [c[0], c[1], c[2]]), ['', 'PRECONDITION', 'DETAIL']));
  print('');

  const nextRelease = deployIsTag
    ? '  Release is a SEPARATE human step: git tag vX.Y.Z && git push origin vX.Y.Z'
    : deploy === 'manual'
      ? `  Deploy is a SEPARATE human step and this command does NOT perform it: promoting moves main only.\n  Production stays on the old code until a human runs the runbook${doc.runbook ? ` (${doc.runbook})` : ' — and project.yml names none'}.`
      : '  (deploy: push-main — this promotion IS the deploy; there is no separate tag step)';

  if (opts.dry) {
    print(preOk ? '→ READY: preconditions pass. Re-run without --dry to promote.' : '→ NOT READY: fix the ✗ rows above.');
    print(`  Plan: git merge --no-ff ${trunk} into ${MAIN} · push ${MAIN} (COLAB_PROMOTE=1)`);
    print(nextRelease);
    return preOk ? 0 : 1;
  }
  if (!preOk) { process.stderr.write('✗ Aborting: one or more preconditions failed (see ✗ above). Nothing was pushed.\n'); return 1; }

  // ---- ACTION: merge --no-ff trunk → main, push main (never tag, never anything else) ----
  let mainDir = useRepoDir ? repoAbs : null, ephemeral = false;
  if (!mainDir) {
    mainDir = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'colab-promote-'));
    const add = git.git(['worktree', 'add', mainDir, MAIN], repoAbs);
    if (!add.ok) { try { fs.rmdirSync(mainDir); } catch (_) {} throw new UserError(`could not check out main for promotion: ${add.stderr.split('\n')[0]}`); }
    ephemeral = true;
  }
  const cleanup = () => { if (ephemeral) git.git(['worktree', 'remove', '--force', mainDir], repoAbs); };

  const msg = opts.message || `release: ${trunk} → main — ${today()} (promotion via colab promote)`;
  print(`merge --no-ff ${trunk} → ${MAIN} …`);
  const merge = git.run('git', ['merge', '--no-ff', trunk, '-m', msg], { cwd: mainDir });
  if (!merge.ok) {
    git.git(['merge', '--abort'], mainDir); cleanup();
    process.stderr.write(`✗ merge --no-ff failed: ${merge.stderr.split('\n')[0]}. Nothing pushed.\n`);
    return 1;
  }
  const sha = git.git(['rev-parse', '--short', 'HEAD'], mainDir).stdout;

  print(`push ${MAIN} …`);
  const push = git.run('git', ['push', 'origin', MAIN], { cwd: mainDir, env: { ...process.env, COLAB_PROMOTE: '1' } });
  if (!push.ok) {
    cleanup();
    process.stderr.write(`✗ push failed: ${push.stderr.split('\n')[0]}. main merged locally at ${sha} but NOT pushed — resolve and push by hand (COLAB_HUMAN=1).\n`);
    return 1;
  }
  cleanup();

  print('');
  print(`✓ Promoted ${trunk} → ${MAIN} @ ${sha}`);

  if (deploy === 'manual') {
    manualDeployBlock(doc.runbook, MAIN).forEach(print);
    return 0;
  }

  print(deployIsTag
    ? '  main CI now runs the heavy verification suite (no deploy — deploy is by tag).'
    : '  main push will now DEPLOY to production (deploy: push-main).');
  print(nextRelease);
  return 0;
}

